/* Generated by build_study.js: modules, glossary, mini exam banks and the prof's review questions. */
window.STUDY={"modules":[{"id":"m1","n":1,"file":"module-1.html","title":"Introduction to Cell Biology and the Cell Theory","short":"The cell theory","progressKey":"biol130-m1-progress-v1","blurb":"Where cell biology started: the first microscopes, the cell theory, and what does and doesn't count as a cell.","topics":["Hooke, Leeuwenhoek and the first microscopes","The three tenets of the cell theory","Viruses, viroids and prions","Prokaryotic vs eukaryotic, plant vs animal cells","Model organisms"],"ready":true,"chapters":[{"title":"Seeing cells","pages":"p. 4","blurb":"What a cell is, what Hooke and Leeuwenhoek saw, and the three kinds of microscopy.","summary":"Cells are the basic unit of life, and almost all are too small to see without a microscope. Hooke saw empty walls; Leeuwenhoek saw living cells. Next: how those observations became a theory."},{"title":"The cell theory","pages":"p. 5","blurb":"Schleiden, Schwann and Virchow, and the three tenets of the cell theory.","summary":"All living things are made of cells, the cell is the unit of life, and cells come only from other cells. Next: the things that break the rule."},{"title":"Not quite alive","pages":"p. 5","blurb":"Viruses, viroids and prions: the exceptions to the cell theory.","summary":"Viruses are nucleic acid in a protein coat; viroids are bare circles of RNA; prions are protein only. None can reproduce without a living cell. Next: what every real cell has."},{"title":"What every cell has","pages":"p. 6","blurb":"The basic properties of cells, cytoplasm vs cytosol, the central dogma and ATP.","summary":"Every cell has a membrane, a genetic program read through the central dogma, and runs on energy carried by ATP. Next: the two kinds of cells."},{"title":"Prokaryotes and the tree of life","pages":"p. 7","blurb":"Three domains, two kinds of cell, and what a bacterium is made of.","summary":"Prokaryotes (Bacteria and Archaea) have no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles, a single circular chromosome, and divide by binary fission. Next: the eukaryotes."},{"title":"The eukaryotes","pages":"pp. 8–9","blurb":"Protists, fungi, plants and animals, and what sets plant cells apart.","summary":"Protists are diverse and mostly single-celled; fungi have chitin walls and absorb food; plants have walls, vacuoles, chloroplasts and plasmodesmata; animals have no walls. Next: the organelles inside."},{"title":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","pages":"p. 9","blurb":"The endomembrane system, the organelles outside it, and what each one does.","summary":"The endomembrane system builds, modifies and ships materials; mitochondria, chloroplasts, peroxisomes and ribosomes work outside it. Next: the organisms scientists use to study all this."},{"title":"Model organisms","pages":"p. 10","blurb":"Why biologists study bacteria, yeast, cress, flies, worms and mice.","summary":"Model organisms are non-human species that are easy to study and tell us about biology in general. Last: build four cells."},{"title":"Build a cell","pages":"pp. 3–11","blurb":"The finale: assemble a bacterium, an animal cell, a plant cell and a yeast cell.","summary":"You can now build any of the four cell types from their parts, and explain why each part belongs. Try the \\"}]},{"id":"m2","n":2,"file":"module-2.html","title":"Molecules of Life","short":"Molecules of life","progressKey":"biol130-m2-progress-v1","blurb":"The chemistry of life, from atoms and bonds to water, carbon and the four kinds of macromolecules.","topics":["Atoms, valence electrons and bonds","Water, polarity and pH","Carbon and functional groups","Condensation and hydrolysis","Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, nucleic acids"],"ready":true,"chapters":[{"title":"Atoms","pages":"p. 13","blurb":"Elements, protons, neutrons, electrons, orbitals and valence electrons.","summary":"An atom is a nucleus of protons and neutrons with electrons around it. The valence electrons in the outer shell decide how many bonds it makes. Next: the bonds themselves."},{"title":"Bonds","pages":"pp. 14–15","blurb":"Covalent bonds, polar and nonpolar, electronegativity, and ionic bonds.","summary":"Equal sharing gives a nonpolar covalent bond, unequal sharing a polar one, and stealing an electron gives ions held by an ionic bond. Next: water, the molecule that makes polarity matter."},{"title":"Water","pages":"pp. 15–16","blurb":"Chemical reactions, hydrogen bonds, dissolving salt, and hydrophilic vs hydrophobic.","summary":"Water is polar, so its molecules hydrogen-bond to each other, pull ions apart, and push nonpolar molecules together. Next: how acidic or basic a solution is."},{"title":"pH","pages":"p. 17","blurb":"Hydrogen ions, the pH scale, acids and bases.","summary":"pH measures H⁺: below 7 is acidic, above 7 basic, and each step is tenfold. Next: carbon, the backbone of organic molecules."},{"title":"Carbon and functional groups","pages":"pp. 18, 20","blurb":"Why carbon is the backbone of life, and the functional groups that give molecules their character.","summary":"Carbon makes four bonds, forming chains and rings. Functional groups with N, O, P or S are polar; methyl is nonpolar. Next: how small molecules become macromolecules."},{"title":"Building macromolecules","pages":"p. 19","blurb":"The four kinds of biological molecule, monomers and polymers, condensation and hydrolysis.","summary":"Condensation links monomers and releases water; hydrolysis uses water to break them apart. Next: the first of the four, proteins."},{"title":"Proteins","pages":"pp. 21–22","blurb":"What proteins do, amino acid structure, zwitterions and peptide bonds.","summary":"Amino acids share a backbone and differ only in their R groups; peptide bonds join them into polypeptides. Next: nucleic acids."},{"title":"Nucleic acids","pages":"pp. 23–25","blurb":"Nucleotides and nucleosides, purines and pyrimidines, DNA vs RNA, phosphodiester bonds and ATP.","summary":"A nucleotide is a base, a sugar and a phosphate; phosphodiester bonds link them into strands that grow 5′ → 3′. Nucleotides also carry energy (ATP), signal (cAMP) and help enzymes (NAD). Next: carbohydrates."},{"title":"Carbohydrates","pages":"pp. 26–28","blurb":"Monosaccharides and isomers, ring forms, glycosidic bonds, and di-, oligo- and polysaccharides.","summary":"Monosaccharides close into rings, link by glycosidic bonds, and build up into disaccharides, oligosaccharides and polysaccharides for energy or structure. Next: lipids."},{"title":"Lipids","pages":"pp. 29–30","blurb":"Triacylglycerols, saturated and unsaturated fatty acids, steroids and phospholipids.","summary":"Lipids are hydrophobic and not polymers: triacylglycerols store energy, steroids like cholesterol sit in membranes and make hormones, and amphipathic phospholipids build membranes. Last: the finale."},{"title":"Name that molecule","pages":"pp. 12–31","blurb":"The finale: ten mystery molecules. Read the clues and name them.","summary":"From atoms to macromolecules: you can now read a molecule's makeup and say what kind it is. Try the chemistry desk for endless practice, or take the mini exam."}]},{"id":"m3","n":3,"file":"module-3.html","title":"Cell Border Control: Cells and Membranes","short":"Cells and membranes","progressKey":"cbc-progress-v1","blurb":"Follow glucose from the gut into the blood through one intestinal cell.","topics":["Organelles and the endomembrane system","Mitochondria, chloroplasts and endosymbiosis","Membrane structure and fluidity","Osmosis and tonicity","Passive, active and bulk transport"],"ready":true,"chapters":[{"title":"Your cell","pages":"pp. 37–40","blurb":"The parts of an animal cell, and how it ships a new protein to its membrane.","summary":"You know your cell's parts and how a new membrane protein travels from the nucleus, through the ER and Golgi, to the plasma membrane. Next you will look closely at the membrane itself."},{"title":"Power plants","pages":"pp. 41–42","blurb":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts: how their structure fits their job, and the endosymbiont theory of where they came from.","summary":"Mitochondria make ATP and chloroplasts capture light energy. Both have two membranes, their own DNA and ribosomes, and divide on their own: leftovers of the bacteria they once were. Next: the border itself."},{"title":"The border","pages":"pp. 33–34, 36","blurb":"The phospholipid bilayer, its proteins, and what can cross it unaided.","summary":"Your border is a hydrophobic core with protein gates set into it. Small nonpolar molecules cross on their own; almost everything else needs a protein. Next: what water does."},{"title":"Membrane chemistry","pages":"pp. 33–36","blurb":"Why phospholipids form bilayers on their own, what controls fluidity, and what membrane proteins and carbohydrates do.","summary":"Phospholipids assemble into self-sealing bilayers because water excludes their tails. Tail length, double bonds, cholesterol and temperature set how fluid the membrane is. Next: what water does."},{"title":"Water","pages":"p. 45","blurb":"Osmosis, osmotic pressure, and what isotonic, hypotonic and hypertonic do to a cell.","summary":"Water follows solute. Compare the outside to the cell, name the solution, and you can predict what the cell does. Next: moving solutes down their gradients."},{"title":"Downhill","pages":"pp. 43–44","blurb":"Passive transport: simple diffusion, channels and carriers.","summary":"Passive transport runs downhill. The bilayer handles small nonpolar molecules, channels handle ions and water, and carriers handle bigger polar molecules like glucose. Next: going uphill."},{"title":"The pump","pages":"pp. 46–47","blurb":"Primary active transport, the Na⁺/K⁺ pump and electrochemical gradients.","summary":"The Na⁺/K⁺ pump spends ATP to keep Na⁺ low inside the cell. That stored gradient is the battery for the next chapter."},{"title":"The glucose run","pages":"pp. 47–48","blurb":"Secondary active transport, symport and antiport, and the full route from gut to blood.","summary":"You built the whole route. Na⁺ flowing in pays for glucose to come in uphill; glucose leaves downhill; the pump resets the Na⁺ gradient with ATP. That is the core of this unit."},{"title":"Bulk traffic","pages":"pp. 49–50","blurb":"Exocytosis, endocytosis, phagocytosis, pinocytosis and receptor-mediated endocytosis.","summary":"Some cargo is too big or too plentiful for any transporter. It travels in vesicles instead, and that costs energy."},{"title":"Full meal","pages":"pp. 32–51","blurb":"The finale. Every system runs at once and each call counts.","summary":"You ran the border through a whole meal. Replay any chapter to push your first-try score up, or head to Checkpoint for endless practice."}]},{"id":"m4","n":4,"file":"module-4.html","title":"Nucleic Acids and Information Flow","short":"Nucleic acids","progressKey":"biol130-m4-progress-v1","blurb":"Take a gene from its enhancer to a finished mRNA.","topics":["Griffith and Avery: DNA as the genetic material","Nucleotides and the double helix","The central dogma","Transcription in prokaryotes and eukaryotes","RNA processing and alternative splicing"],"ready":true,"chapters":[{"title":"The genetic material","pages":"pp. 53–54","blurb":"How Griffith, then Avery, MacLeod and McCarty showed that DNA carries genetic information.","summary":"Griffith showed that something in dead cells could transform living ones. Avery's group showed that something was DNA. Next: what DNA is made of."},{"title":"Nucleotides and phosphodiester bonds","pages":"p. 55","blurb":"The three parts of a nucleotide, the four bases, and how a strand gets its 5′ and 3′ ends.","summary":"A strand is a chain of nucleotides with a 5′ phosphate at one end and a 3′–OH at the other, and it only grows from the 3′ end. Next: two strands together."},{"title":"The double helix","pages":"pp. 53, 56–57","blurb":"Antiparallel strands, complementary base pairs, hydrogen bonds, base stacking and packing into chromosomes.","summary":"Two antiparallel strands, held by weak hydrogen bonds and base stacking, wound into a right-handed helix and packed by histones. Next: how the cell reads it."},{"title":"Gene expression and the central dogma","pages":"pp. 58–59","blurb":"Genomes, genes, gene expression, the central dogma and where transcription happens.","summary":"Genes are transcribed into RNA and translated into protein, in the cytoplasm for prokaryotes and across two compartments for eukaryotes. Next: RNA itself."},{"title":"RNA","pages":"p. 60","blurb":"How RNA differs from DNA: sugar, base, 5′ end, size and shape.","summary":"RNA is a less stable, usually single-stranded cousin of DNA, with ribose and uracil. Next: how RNA is made."},{"title":"Starting transcription","pages":"pp. 61–63","blurb":"What transcription needs, promoters and the TATA box, sigma factor, general transcription factors, enhancers and the mediator.","summary":"Transcription starts at a promoter. Bacteria use a sigma factor; eukaryotes assemble general transcription factors, activators, mediator and Pol II through DNA looping. Next: elongation."},{"title":"Elongation","pages":"pp. 61, 63–64","blurb":"Driving RNA polymerase along the template, and the chemistry of adding each nucleotide.","summary":"The template is read 3′→5′, the RNA grows 5′→3′, and every nucleotide is paid for by its own triphosphate. Next: how prokaryotes and eukaryotes differ."},{"title":"Prokaryotic vs eukaryotic transcription","pages":"p. 65","blurb":"Coupled transcription and translation, polycistronic mRNA, and why eukaryotes need processing.","summary":"Prokaryotes translate as they transcribe, and one mRNA can carry several genes. Eukaryotes separate the two with a nucleus, so the transcript has to be processed. Next: processing."},{"title":"RNA processing and alternative splicing","pages":"pp. 65–66","blurb":"The 5′ cap, the poly(A) tail, RNA splicing, and one gene making more than one protein.","summary":"A capped, spliced, polyadenylated mRNA is ready to leave the nucleus, and alternative splicing lets one gene make several proteins. Last: the finale."},{"title":"Express the gene","pages":"pp. 52–67","blurb":"The finale: take one gene from enhancer to ribosome, one call at a time.","summary":"You took a gene from its enhancer to the ribosome. Replay any chapter to raise your first-try score, or practise at the sequence desk."}]},{"id":"m5","n":5,"file":"module-5.html","title":"Protein Structure, Function and Synthesis","short":"Proteins","progressKey":"biol130-m5-progress-v1","ready":false,"blurb":"Amino acids, the four levels of protein structure, the genetic code and translation. Waiting on the Module 5 notes.","topics":["Levels of protein structure","The genetic code, codons and anticodons","Translation: initiation, elongation, termination","Protein targeting and signal sequences"],"chapters":[]}],"glossary":[{"k":"cell","mod":"m1","n":"Cell","d":"The fundamental, basic unit of life. Every organism is made of either a single cell or a group of cells.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"hooke","mod":"m1","n":"Robert Hooke (1665)","d":"Looked at thin slices of cork and described its chambers as \"cells\". He was really looking at empty walls, the remains of dead cells.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"leeuwenhoek","mod":"m1","n":"Anton van Leeuwenhoek (1673–1700)","d":"An early light microscopist who improved the magnifying power of microscope lenses. He saw living \"animalcules\" swimming in pond water.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"light-microscopy","mod":"m1","n":"Light microscopy","d":"Uses visible light and glass lenses. Good for whole cells and tissues, including living ones; it cannot show the smallest structures.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"electron-microscopy","mod":"m1","n":"Electron microscopy","d":"Uses a beam of electrons instead of light, so it can show much finer detail, such as the surface of bacteria or structures smaller than a micrometre.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"fluorescence-microscopy","mod":"m1","n":"Fluorescence microscopy","d":"Uses dyes that glow to label specific parts of a cell. In your notes, DAPI stains the nucleus blue and phalloidin stains actin.","p":"4","g":"Seeing cells","ch":0},{"k":"cell-theory","mod":"m1","n":"The cell theory","d":"A pillar of modern biology, with three tenets: 1. All living organisms are composed of one (unicellular) or more (multicellular) cells. 2. Each cell is the structural and functional unit of life. 3. Cells can arise only from pre-existing cells.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"schleiden","mod":"m1","n":"Schleiden","d":"A botanist who noted that all plant tissues are composed of cells and that plant embryos arise from a single cell. With Schwann, proposed the first two tenets.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"schwann","mod":"m1","n":"Schwann","d":"A zoologist who came to the same conclusion about animals, and saw that plants and animals are similar. With Schleiden, proposed the first two tenets.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"virchow","mod":"m1","n":"Virchow","d":"A physician who added the third tenet of the cell theory: cells can arise only from pre-existing cells.","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"unicellular","mod":"m1","n":"Unicellular and multicellular","d":"A unicellular organism is a single cell (a bacterium, a yeast cell). A multicellular organism is made of many cells (a plant, a mouse).","p":"5","g":"The cell theory","ch":1},{"k":"exception","mod":"m1","n":"Exceptions to the cell theory","d":"Viruses, viroids and prions. They are not made of cells and cannot reproduce on their own, yet they can be copied inside living cells.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"virus","mod":"m1","n":"Virus","d":"A small amount of nucleic acid (DNA or RNA) inside a protein coat, sometimes with an outer envelope. Outside a living cell it is inert; it needs a host cell to reproduce.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"capsid","mod":"m1","n":"Capsid","d":"The protein coat of a virus, which surrounds its genome.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"viral-genome","mod":"m1","n":"Viral genome","d":"The virus's genetic material, DNA or RNA, packed inside the capsid.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"viroid","mod":"m1","n":"Viroid","d":"A small circular RNA with no protein coat. It needs the host's machinery to replicate. Example: potato spindle tuber viroid.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"prion","mod":"m1","n":"Prion","d":"A proteinaceous infectious particle with NO nucleic acid. A misfolded form of the prion protein (PrP^Sc) makes the normal form (PrP^C) misfold too, and the misfolded protein piles up, leaving holes in brain tissue.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"host-cell","mod":"m1","n":"Host cell","d":"A living cell that a virus or viroid uses to copy itself. It supplies the ribosomes, enzymes and energy the virus lacks.","p":"5","g":"Not quite cells","ch":2},{"k":"cell-properties","mod":"m1","n":"Basic properties of cells","d":"Cells are highly complex and organized, enclosed by a plasma membrane; have a genetic program (DNA); can reproduce; acquire and use energy; carry out chemical reactions; do mechanical work; respond to stimuli; and regulate themselves.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"plasma-membrane","mod":"m1","n":"Cell (plasma) membrane","d":"The physical barrier that encloses every cell and separates it from the outside. It controls what goes in and out.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"cytoplasm","mod":"m1","n":"Cytoplasm","d":"All the contents found inside the cell membrane. (Module 3 refines this: the contents minus the nucleus.)","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"cytosol","mod":"m1","n":"Cytosol","d":"The jelly-like internal fluid of a cell: the cytoplasm minus the organelles. It contains water, dissolved ions and molecules.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"nucleic-acids","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleic acids (DNA, RNA)","d":"The information molecules of the cell. DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) stores the genetic program; RNA (ribonucleic acid) carries it to be used. Covered in detail in Modules 2 and 4.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"dna","mod":"m1","n":"DNA","d":"Deoxyribonucleic acid, the genetic material of every cell. Its genes are transcribed into RNA.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"rna","mod":"m1","n":"RNA","d":"Ribonucleic acid. Made from DNA by transcription, then translated into protein.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"protein","mod":"m1","n":"Protein","d":"The functional product of most genes, made by translation of RNA. Proteins do most of the work in a cell.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"gene","mod":"m1","n":"Gene","d":"The DNA code for the information needed to produce a functional product, such as a protein.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"central-dogma","mod":"m1","n":"The central dogma","d":"The flow of genetic information: DNA is transcribed into RNA, and RNA is translated into protein.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"transcription","mod":"m1","n":"Transcription","d":"Making RNA from a DNA template. The \"language\" stays the same (nucleic acid to nucleic acid).","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"translation","mod":"m1","n":"Translation","d":"Making a protein from the information in RNA. The language changes from nucleotides to amino acids.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"bioenergetics","mod":"m1","n":"Bioenergetics","d":"How cells acquire and use energy. Example: plants convert light energy into chemical energy.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"metabolism","mod":"m1","n":"Metabolism","d":"All the chemical reactions in a cell, organized into metabolic pathways such as glycolysis and the citric acid cycle.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"atp","mod":"m1","n":"Adenosine triphosphate (ATP)","d":"The cell's energy currency: adenine + ribose + three phosphate groups. The bonds linking the phosphate groups have high potential energy. With one phosphate it is AMP, with two ADP.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"adenine","mod":"m1","n":"Adenine","d":"The nitrogen-containing base in ATP. Adenine plus ribose is adenosine.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"ribose","mod":"m1","n":"Ribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in ATP (and in RNA).","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"phosphate-group","mod":"m1","n":"Phosphate groups (AMP, ADP, ATP)","d":"Adenosine with one phosphate is AMP, with two is ADP, with three is ATP. The bonds between phosphates carry high potential energy.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":null},{"k":"self-regulation","mod":"m1","n":"Self-regulation and response to stimuli","d":"Cells respond to signals using receptors, and control their own activities. A cell that loses self-regulation can grow uncontrollably.","p":"6","g":"What all cells share","ch":3},{"k":"prokaryote","mod":"m1","n":"Prokaryote","d":"\"Before nucleus\". Single-celled organisms with no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles. The most abundant organisms on Earth. Two domains: Bacteria and Archaea.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"eukaryote","mod":"m1","n":"Eukaryote","d":"\"True nucleus\". Cells with a membrane-bound nucleus and many organelles. Domain Eukarya: protists, fungi, plants and animals.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"tree-of-life","mod":"m1","n":"Tree of life","d":"The evolutionary tree of all organisms, with three domains: Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya. Most of life's diversity is microbial.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"bacteria","mod":"m1","n":"Domain Bacteria","d":"Prokaryotes with a single circular chromosome. Many have plasmids, and they have a cell wall surrounding the cell membrane.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"archaea","mod":"m1","n":"Domain Archaea","d":"Prokaryotes with a single circular chromosome and an isoprenoid cell membrane. They often inhabit extreme environments. Eukaryotes are thought to have descended from within the Archaea.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"eukarya","mod":"m1","n":"Domain Eukarya","d":"All eukaryotes. Plants and animals lie on branches within the Eukarya.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"nucleoid","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleoid","d":"The region of a prokaryotic cell where its single circular chromosome sits. It is not enclosed by a membrane.","p":"6–7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"plasmid","mod":"m1","n":"Plasmid","d":"A small circle of extra DNA found in many bacteria, separate from the main chromosome.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"cell-wall","mod":"m1","n":"Cell wall","d":"A rigid layer outside the plasma membrane. Bacteria have one; fungal walls contain chitin; plant walls are made of polysaccharides. Animal cells have none.","p":"7, 9","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"flagellum","mod":"m1","n":"Flagellum (plural flagella)","d":"A long whip-like structure some cells use to move.","p":"6–7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"ribosome","mod":"m1","n":"Ribosome","d":"The machine that makes proteins (translation). Found in every cell, prokaryotic and eukaryotic. It has no membrane, so it is not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"7, 9","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"binary-fission","mod":"m1","n":"Binary fission","d":"How prokaryotes reproduce: one cell copies its DNA and divides into two.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"bacterial-shapes","mod":"m1","n":"Bacterial shapes","d":"Spheres (Streptococcus, in strings), rods (E. coli), helices (Streptomyces). Sizes range up to Thiomargarita namibiensis, the largest known bacterial cell. Myxobacteria aggregate into fruiting bodies.","p":"7","g":"Prokaryotes","ch":4},{"k":"organelle","mod":"m1","n":"Organelle","d":"A specialized structure inside a eukaryotic cell that carries out a particular job, such as the nucleus, mitochondria or Golgi apparatus.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"protists","mod":"m1","n":"Protists","d":"A diverse group of eukaryotes, mostly single cells but some form colonies. Includes algae (Volvox), water molds (potato blight), slime molds and protozoa (ciliates).","p":"8","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"fungi","mod":"m1","n":"Fungi","d":"Eukaryotes with cell walls containing chitin. Includes yeasts, rusts, smuts, mildews, molds and mushrooms. They are heterotrophs that absorb food directly from the environment.","p":"8","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"chitin","mod":"m1","n":"Chitin","d":"A modified polysaccharide found in fungal cell walls.","p":"8","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"heterotroph","mod":"m1","n":"Heterotroph","d":"An organism that depends on preformed organic molecules for its carbon and energy. Fungi and animals are heterotrophs.","p":"8–9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"autotroph","mod":"m1","n":"Autotroph","d":"An organism that produces its own food. Plants use chloroplasts to convert the sun's energy into chemical energy.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"plants","mod":"m1","n":"Plants","d":"Multicellular autotrophs with cell walls made of polysaccharides, large water-filled vacuoles, chloroplasts, and connections between cells called plasmodesmata.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"animals","mod":"m1","n":"Animals","d":"Multicellular heterotrophs with no cell walls, which gain energy and carbon from preformed organic molecules and have a distinct mode of early development.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"vacuole","mod":"m1","n":"Vacuole","d":"A membrane-bound sac. Plant cells have a large water-filled vacuole that gives structural rigidity by contributing to turgor pressure.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"plasmodesmata","mod":"m1","n":"Plasmodesmata","d":"Channels through the cell wall that connect neighbouring plant cells.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"chloroplast","mod":"m1","n":"Chloroplast","d":"The organelle where plant cells harness the energy of sunlight to make sugars. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Eukaryotes","ch":5},{"k":"nucleus","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleus and nuclear envelope","d":"The nucleus is the membrane-bound storehouse for the cell's chromosomes and the site of RNA synthesis. The nuclear envelope around it is part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"nucleolus","mod":"m1","n":"Nucleolus","d":"A dense region inside the nucleus where ribosome parts are made. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"nuclear-pores","mod":"m1","n":"Nuclear pores","d":"Openings in the nuclear envelope that let molecules such as RNA move between the nucleus and the cytoplasm. (More in Module 3, p. 38.)","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":null},{"k":"endomembrane","mod":"m1","n":"Endomembrane system (EMS)","d":"Builds, modifies and transports cellular materials. Includes the nuclear envelope, rough and smooth ER, Golgi apparatus, lysosomes, vesicles and the plasma membrane.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"non-endomembrane","mod":"m1","n":"Non-endomembrane structures","d":"Organelles that are not part of the EMS: mitochondria, chloroplasts, peroxisomes, ribosomes, the nucleolus, cytoskeleton, centrosomes and cilia/flagella.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"er","mod":"m1","n":"Endoplasmic reticulum (smooth and rough)","d":"A network of membranes involved in both protein and lipid synthesis. Rough ER has ribosomes attached; smooth ER does not.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"golgi","mod":"m1","n":"Golgi apparatus","d":"Modifies and sorts proteins and lipids as they move to their final destinations inside or outside the cell.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"lysosome","mod":"m1","n":"Lysosome","d":"Contains enzymes that break down macromolecules such as proteins, nucleic acids, lipids and carbohydrates.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"vesicle","mod":"m1","n":"Vesicle","d":"A small membrane sac that carries material between parts of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"mitochondria","mod":"m1","n":"Mitochondrion","d":"Produces most of the ATP that serves as the energy currency of the cell. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"peroxisome","mod":"m1","n":"Peroxisome","d":"Breaks down specific organic molecules such as fatty acids, and synthesizes others such as cholesterol and some phospholipids. Not part of the endomembrane system.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"cytoskeleton","mod":"m1","n":"Cytoskeleton","d":"A network of protein filaments and associated proteins that gives the cell an internal structural framework.","p":"9","g":"Inside a eukaryotic cell","ch":6},{"k":"model-organism","mod":"m1","n":"Model organism","d":"A non-human species studied to understand biology in general. Your notes list E. coli, yeast, Arabidopsis, Drosophila, C. elegans and the mouse.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"ecoli","mod":"m1","n":"Escherichia coli (bacterium)","d":"Used to study DNA replication, gene transcription and translation.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"yeast","mod":"m1","n":"Saccharomyces cerevisiae (yeast)","d":"Used to study the cell cycle: the \"minimal model eukaryote\". Reproduces by budding.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"arabidopsis","mod":"m1","n":"Arabidopsis thaliana (plant)","d":"A model for flowering plants, which are closely related to each other.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"drosophila","mod":"m1","n":"Drosophila melanogaster (fruit fly)","d":"Used to study genetics and development.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"celegans","mod":"m1","n":"Caenorhabditis elegans (worm)","d":"The first animal genome to be sequenced. The location, lineage and fate of every cell in the embryo, larva and adult is known.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"mouse","mod":"m1","n":"Mus musculus (mouse)","d":"The \"model mammal\", with well understood genetics.","p":"10","g":"Model organisms","ch":7},{"k":"element","mod":"m2","n":"Element","d":"A substance made of only one type of atom. Around a dozen elements make up living things; carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and phosphorus are the most abundant in cells.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"atom","mod":"m2","n":"Atom","d":"The basic unit of matter: a dense central nucleus of protons and neutrons, with electrons moving around it.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"nucleus-atom","mod":"m2","n":"Atomic nucleus","d":"The dense centre of an atom, made of positively charged protons and neutral neutrons.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"proton","mod":"m2","n":"Proton","d":"A positively charged particle in the atomic nucleus. The number of protons decides which element an atom is.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"neutron","mod":"m2","n":"Neutron","d":"A neutral (uncharged) particle in the atomic nucleus.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"electron","mod":"m2","n":"Electron","d":"A negatively charged particle that moves around the nucleus in orbitals.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"orbital","mod":"m2","n":"Orbital","d":"The region of space around the nucleus where electrons move. An orbital holds at most 2 electrons.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"valence","mod":"m2","n":"Valence electrons","d":"Electrons in the outermost orbitals of an atom. Their number determines how many bonds the atom can make.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"molecule","mod":"m2","n":"Molecule","d":"Two or more atoms combined by chemical bonds, for example H₂, CH₄ or H₂O.","p":"13","g":"Atoms","ch":0},{"k":"covalent-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Covalent bond","d":"A bond in which atoms share valence electrons in a molecular orbital. Covalent bonds are the strong bonds in biological systems.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"electronegativity","mod":"m2","n":"Electronegativity","d":"How strongly an atom attracts shared electrons. Oxygen and nitrogen are highly electronegative; hydrogen and carbon much less so.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"polar-covalent","mod":"m2","n":"Polar covalent bond","d":"Unequal sharing of electrons: the more electronegative atom pulls them closer and gets a slight negative charge (δ⁻), leaving the other slightly positive (δ⁺). The O–H bonds in water are polar.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"nonpolar-covalent","mod":"m2","n":"Nonpolar covalent bond","d":"Equal sharing of electrons between atoms with the same, or nearly the same, electronegativity, as in H–H or C–H.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"polar","mod":"m2","n":"Polar","d":"Having an uneven distribution of charge. Water is a polar molecule: the oxygen is slightly negative and the two hydrogens slightly positive, giving it an angular (bent) shape.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"nonpolar","mod":"m2","n":"Nonpolar","d":"Having an even distribution of charge, so there are no partial charges. Hydrocarbons such as oils and the methyl group are nonpolar.","p":"14","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"ionic-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Ionic bond","d":"Forms when one atom \"steals\" an electron from another because of a large difference in electronegativity. The resulting ions are held together by opposite charges, as in NaCl.","p":"15","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"cation","mod":"m2","n":"Cation","d":"A positively charged ion: it has an extra proton relative to its number of electrons. Na⁺ is a cation.","p":"15","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"anion","mod":"m2","n":"Anion","d":"A negatively charged ion: it has an extra electron relative to its number of protons. Cl⁻ is an anion.","p":"15","g":"Bonds","ch":1},{"k":"chemical-reaction","mod":"m2","n":"Chemical reaction","d":"The breaking and forming of chemical bonds, which transforms starting substances into different molecules.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"reactants","mod":"m2","n":"Reactants","d":"The starting substances in a chemical reaction.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"products","mod":"m2","n":"Products","d":"The new molecules formed by a chemical reaction.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"solvent","mod":"m2","n":"Water as a solvent","d":"Water is the medium of life and the most abundant molecule in organisms. It dissolves more kinds of molecules than any other liquid, thanks to its polarity.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"hydrogen-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrogen bond","d":"A weak bond between an electronegative atom (such as O or N) and a hydrogen atom that is already covalently bonded to another electronegative atom. Water molecules hydrogen-bond to each other.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"hydrophilic","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrophilic","d":"\"Water loving\": highly attracted to water. Polar and charged molecules, such as NaCl or sugars, are hydrophilic and dissolve.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"hydrophobic","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrophobic","d":"\"Water fearing\": not attracted to water. Nonpolar molecules such as oils clump together in water.","p":"16","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"amphipathic","mod":"m2","n":"Amphipathic (amphiphilic)","d":"Having both a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic region. Phospholipids are amphipathic, an important feature in biology: in water they form micelles and bilayers.","p":"16, 30","g":"Water","ch":2},{"k":"ph","mod":"m2","n":"pH","d":"A measure of the concentration of protons (H⁺) in a solution, on a scale from 0 to 14. Pure water is pH 7. pH matters for cell function, such as protein structure and enzyme activity.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"acid","mod":"m2","n":"Acidic","d":"A solution with pH lower than 7: more H⁺ than pure water. Lemon juice (pH 2) and stomach fluid are acidic.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"base","mod":"m2","n":"Basic","d":"A solution with pH higher than 7: less H⁺ than pure water. Baking soda (pH 9) and bleach are basic.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"hydrogen-ion","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrogen ion (H⁺)","d":"A proton. A small proportion of water molecules dissociate into H⁺ and OH⁻.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"hydroxide","mod":"m2","n":"Hydroxide ion (OH⁻)","d":"The other product when a water molecule dissociates.","p":"17","g":"Water","ch":3},{"k":"organic-molecule","mod":"m2","n":"Organic molecule","d":"A molecule that contains carbon. With water removed, the human body is mostly carbon (52% of dry mass), oxygen, hydrogen and nitrogen.","p":"18","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"carbon","mod":"m2","n":"Carbon","d":"Forms four covalent bonds, single or double, so it can link into chains and rings with many other elements. In methane its four bonds point to the corners of a tetrahedron.","p":"18","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"functional-group","mod":"m2","n":"Functional group","d":"A group of atoms attached to a carbon chain that gives it chemical properties of its own. Groups with N, O, P or S are polar; the methyl group is nonpolar. They take part in reactions that build polymers.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-amino","mod":"m2","n":"Amino group (–NH₂)","d":"Polar, positively charged at the pH of a cell, behaves as a base, hydrophilic. Found in amino acids and proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-amide","mod":"m2","n":"Amide group","d":"Polar and hydrophilic. Found in proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-carboxyl","mod":"m2","n":"Carboxyl group (–COOH)","d":"Polar, negatively charged at the pH of a cell, behaves as an acid, hydrophilic. Found in fatty acids, amino acids and proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-carbonyl","mod":"m2","n":"Carbonyl group (>C=O)","d":"Polar and hydrophilic. Found in carbohydrates and proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-hydroxyl","mod":"m2","n":"Hydroxyl group (–OH)","d":"Polar and hydrophilic. Found in carbohydrates, proteins and nucleic acids.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-sulfhydryl","mod":"m2","n":"Sulfhydryl group (–SH)","d":"Polar; two of them can form an S–S disulfide bond. Found in the amino acid cysteine and in proteins.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-methyl","mod":"m2","n":"Methyl group (–CH₃)","d":"Nonpolar. Found in amino acids, proteins and nucleic acids.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"fg-phosphate","mod":"m2","n":"Phosphate group","d":"Polar, negatively charged at the pH of a cell, hydrophilic. Found in phospholipids, nucleic acids and ATP.","p":"20","g":"Carbon","ch":4},{"k":"macromolecule","mod":"m2","n":"Macromolecule","d":"A large molecule built from smaller organic molecules. The four types of biological molecules are proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates and lipids.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"polymer","mod":"m2","n":"Polymer","d":"A large molecule made of many repeating building blocks (monomers) joined by covalent bonds.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"monomer","mod":"m2","n":"Monomer","d":"A building block of a polymer: amino acids, nucleotides and monosaccharides.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"condensation","mod":"m2","n":"Condensation reaction","d":"Making the chain. Monomers form a covalent bond and a water molecule is released as a byproduct. Requires energy; sped up by enzymes.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"hydrolysis","mod":"m2","n":"Hydrolysis","d":"Breaking the chain. A water molecule is inserted across the bond, splitting the polymer. Releases energy; sped up by enzymes.","p":"19","g":"Macromolecules","ch":5},{"k":"protein","mod":"m2","n":"Proteins","d":"Polymers of amino acids. They provide structural support and act as catalysts, and have more kinds of functions than any other macromolecule.","p":"19, 21","g":"Proteins","ch":5},{"k":"protein-functions","mod":"m2","n":"Functions of proteins","d":"Enzymes (catalysts), transport (across membranes, in circulation), signalling and regulation (hormones, receptors, messengers), movement (contractile proteins, flagella, motor proteins), support (cytoskeleton, extracellular matrix) and defense (antibodies, complement).","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"amino-acid","mod":"m2","n":"Amino acid","d":"The monomer of proteins. A central α carbon bonded to a carboxyl group, an amino group, a hydrogen and an R group (side chain). There are 20 genetically encoded amino acids.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"alpha-carbon","mod":"m2","n":"α (alpha) carbon","d":"The central carbon of an amino acid, covalently linked to four groups: carboxyl, amino, hydrogen and R group.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"r-group","mod":"m2","n":"R group (side chain)","d":"The part of an amino acid that distinguishes one amino acid from another.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"polypeptide","mod":"m2","n":"Polypeptide","d":"A linear polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. Sometimes two or more polypeptide chains combine to form a mature protein.","p":"21","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"zwitterion","mod":"m2","n":"Zwitterion","d":"A molecule with both positive and negative charges. At physiological pH (7.4) an amino acid's amino group is –NH₃⁺ and its carboxyl group is –COO⁻.","p":"22","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"peptide-bond","mod":"m2","n":"Peptide bond","d":"The covalent bond between the carboxyl carbon of one amino acid and the amino nitrogen of the next. As it forms, the carboxyl group gives up an oxygen and the nitrogen two hydrogens, making a water molecule.","p":"22","g":"Proteins","ch":6},{"k":"nucleic-acid","mod":"m2","n":"Nucleic acids (DNA and RNA)","d":"Information molecules that encode and transmit genetic information in their sequence of nucleotides. DNA is the genetic material of all cellular organisms; RNA has many roles, especially in protein synthesis and gene regulation.","p":"19, 23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":5},{"k":"dna","mod":"m2","n":"DNA","d":"Deoxyribonucleic acid: the genetic material in all cellular organisms. Contains the information used to direct protein synthesis. Bases A, G, C, T; sugar deoxyribose.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"rna","mod":"m2","n":"RNA","d":"Ribonucleic acid. A key player in protein synthesis and the regulation of gene expression. Bases A, G, C, U; sugar ribose.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"nucleotide","mod":"m2","n":"Nucleotide","d":"The monomer of nucleic acids, with three parts (nucleoTide has Three parts): a nitrogenous base, a five-carbon sugar, and one or more phosphate groups.","p":"23–24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"nucleoside","mod":"m2","n":"Nucleoside","d":"Just a base and a sugar, with no phosphate (nucleoSide = base and Sugar).","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"nitrogenous-base","mod":"m2","n":"Nitrogenous base","d":"The nitrogen-containing part of a nucleotide, attached to the 1′ carbon of the sugar. The order of bases carries the information in DNA and RNA.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"pyrimidine","mod":"m2","n":"Pyrimidines","d":"Bases with a single ring: cytosine (C), thymine (T) and uracil (U).","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"purine","mod":"m2","n":"Purines","d":"Bases with a double ring: adenine (A) and guanine (G).","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"deoxyribose","mod":"m2","n":"Deoxyribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in DNA. It has just –H on the 2′ carbon.","p":"23–24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"ribose","mod":"m2","n":"Ribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in RNA (and ATP). It has an –OH on the 2′ carbon.","p":"23–24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"adenine","mod":"m2","n":"Adenine","d":"A purine base (two rings), found in DNA, RNA and ATP.","p":"23","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":null},{"k":"phosphate-grp","mod":"m2","n":"Phosphate group (in a nucleotide)","d":"One or more phosphates attached to the 5′ carbon of the sugar. In a chain, a phosphate links the 3′ carbon of one sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next.","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":null},{"k":"nucleotide-functions","mod":"m2","n":"Functions of nucleotides","d":"i. Monomers of DNA and RNA. ii. Signal molecules, such as cyclic AMP (cAMP). iii. Energy transfer: ATP → ADP + Pᵢ + energy. iv. Coenzymes, such as NAD (made with vitamin B3).","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"atp","mod":"m2","n":"ATP","d":"Adenosine triphosphate, a nucleotide. Cleaving off the terminal phosphate releases stored energy: ATP → ADP + Pᵢ + energy.","p":"24","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"phosphodiester","mod":"m2","n":"Phosphodiester bond","d":"The covalent link between the phosphate of one nucleotide and the 3′–OH of the sugar of another. Its formation releases a water molecule and gives the strand direction.","p":"25","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"five-three","mod":"m2","n":"5′ to 3′ direction","d":"The chain begins at the 5′ end (with a phosphate); new nucleotides are added to the free 3′–OH end. Chains are always built 5′ → 3′.","p":"25","g":"Nucleic acids","ch":7},{"k":"carbohydrate","mod":"m2","n":"Carbohydrates","d":"Sugars, made of C, H and O, usually in a 1:2:1 ratio. A major source of energy and also structural molecules, such as cellulose in plants.","p":"19, 26","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":5},{"k":"monosaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Monosaccharide","d":"A simple sugar (mono = one), with 5 or 6 carbons, linear or (more commonly) cyclic. In cells, virtually all are in cyclic form: for glucose, C1 bonds to the O of the –OH on C5.","p":"26","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"isomer","mod":"m2","n":"Isomers (6-carbon sugars)","d":"Molecules with the same formula but a different arrangement. Glucose, galactose and fructose are all C₆H₁₂O₆ but work differently. Galactose differs from glucose at carbon 4; fructose has a ketone group instead of an aldehyde.","p":"26","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"glycosidic","mod":"m2","n":"Glycosidic bond","d":"The covalent link between two monosaccharides, formed between C1 of one and an –OH on a carbon of the other. Forming it releases a water molecule.","p":"27","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"disaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Disaccharide","d":"Two linked monosaccharides (di = two). Sucrose (table sugar, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) is glucose plus fructose.","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"oligosaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Oligosaccharide","d":"A few linked monosaccharides (oligo = few). Attached to proteins they form glycoproteins; attached to lipids, glycolipids.","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"polysaccharide","mod":"m2","n":"Polysaccharide","d":"Many linked monosaccharides (poly = many). Long-term energy storage (starch in plants, glycogen) or structural support (cellulose).","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"glycogen-starch","mod":"m2","n":"Starch and glycogen","d":"Storage forms of glucose. Glycogen is a large, branched chain of glucose attached to a central protein. Starch is a large, branched chain of glucose found in plants such as potatoes, wheat and corn.","p":"28","g":"Carbohydrates","ch":8},{"k":"lipid","mod":"m2","n":"Lipids","d":"A chemically diverse group, and the only macromolecule that is NOT a polymer. Grouped together because they are all hydrophobic. They make up membranes, store energy and help in cell communication.","p":"19, 29","g":"Lipids","ch":5},{"k":"triacylglycerol","mod":"m2","n":"Triacylglycerol","d":"Glycerol joined to three fatty acids. The major component of animal fat and vegetable oil; used for energy storage.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"fatty-acid","mod":"m2","n":"Fatty acid","d":"A long chain of carbons with a carboxyl group (–COOH) at one end.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"glycerol","mod":"m2","n":"Glycerol","d":"A three-carbon molecule with a hydroxyl (–OH) group on each carbon. The backbone of triacylglycerols and phospholipids.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"saturated","mod":"m2","n":"Saturated fatty acid","d":"No double bonds between the carbon atoms, so the chain is straight and packs tightly.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"unsaturated","mod":"m2","n":"Unsaturated fatty acid","d":"Has one or more carbon–carbon double bonds, which put kinks in the chain.","p":"29","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"steroid","mod":"m2","n":"Steroids","d":"Lipids made of many carbon atoms bonded into four characteristic fused rings.","p":"30","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"cholesterol","mod":"m2","n":"Cholesterol","d":"A steroid that is a component of animal cell membranes and the precursor for steroid hormones such as cortisol.","p":"30","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"phospholipid","mod":"m2","n":"Phospholipid","d":"A major component of cell membranes: a glycerol backbone attached to a polar phosphate-containing head group (hydrophilic) and two nonpolar fatty acid tails (hydrophobic). Amphipathic.","p":"30","g":"Lipids","ch":9},{"k":"plasma-membrane","mod":"m3","n":"Plasma membrane","d":"The phospholipid bilayer and its proteins that surround the cell. It decides what gets in and out.","p":"33","g":"Membrane structure","ch":null},{"k":"phospholipid","mod":"m3","n":"Phospholipid","d":"A lipid with a hydrophilic (water-attracting) phosphate head and two hydrophobic fatty acid tails. In water, phospholipids line up into a bilayer with heads facing the water on both sides and tails tucked inside.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"fatty-acid-tails","mod":"m3","n":"Fatty acid tails","d":"The two long hydrocarbon chains of each phospholipid. They are nonpolar, so together they form the oily core of the membrane. That core is what blocks ions and most polar molecules. A kink in an unsaturated tail stops lipids packing tightly and keeps the membrane fluid.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"hydrophilic-head","mod":"m3","n":"Hydrophilic head","d":"The polar phosphate end of a phospholipid. Heads face the watery fluid outside the cell and the cytosol inside.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"bilayer","mod":"m3","n":"Phospholipid bilayer","d":"Two layers of phospholipids, tails pointing inward. The hydrophobic middle is the barrier; proteins set in it are the gates.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"fluid-mosaic","mod":"m3","n":"Fluid mosaic model","d":"The current model of membrane structure. Fluid: lipids and many proteins move sideways within their layer. Mosaic: the membrane is a patchwork of lipids, proteins, cholesterol and carbohydrates.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"selective-permeability","mod":"m3","n":"Selective permeability","d":"The membrane lets some substances through and stops others. Small nonpolar molecules cross the bilayer easily. Water crosses slowly on its own. Ions and large polar molecules need a protein.","p":"43","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"cholesterol","mod":"m3","n":"Cholesterol","d":"A lipid tucked between phospholipids in animal cell membranes. It buffers fluidity: it stops the membrane getting too runny when warm and too stiff when cold.","p":"35","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"integral-protein","mod":"m3","n":"Integral membrane protein","d":"A protein set into the membrane, held there by hydrophobic regions that sit among the fatty acid tails. It can only be removed by breaking the bilayer apart (for example with detergent). Most integral proteins are transmembrane.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"transmembrane-protein","mod":"m3","n":"Transmembrane protein","d":"An integral membrane protein that spans the whole bilayer, with parts on both sides. Every channel and carrier protein is transmembrane, because it has to connect the two sides.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"peripheral-protein","mod":"m3","n":"Peripheral membrane protein","d":"A protein that sits on the surface of the membrane, attached by weak bonds to lipid heads or to integral proteins. It never enters the hydrophobic core, so it can be washed off without breaking the membrane.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"glycoprotein","mod":"m3","n":"Glycoprotein","d":"A membrane protein with a carbohydrate chain attached on the outside surface. Used for cell to cell recognition.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":2},{"k":"glycolipid","mod":"m3","n":"Glycolipid","d":"A membrane lipid with a carbohydrate chain attached, facing outside the cell.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"cytoskeleton","mod":"m3","n":"Cytoskeleton","d":"A network of protein filaments (microtubules, actin filaments, intermediate filaments). It gives the cell its shape, anchors organelles and some membrane proteins, and acts as tracks for vesicles.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"cytoplasm","mod":"m3","n":"Cytoplasm","d":"Everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus: the cytosol plus all the organelles in it.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"cytosol","mod":"m3","n":"Cytosol","d":"The fluid part of the cytoplasm, outside the organelles. Water with dissolved ions, sugars and proteins. Many reactions, including the first stage of breaking down glucose, happen here.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"nucleus","mod":"m3","n":"Nucleus","d":"Holds the cell's DNA. Surrounded by the nuclear envelope, a double membrane that is continuous with the ER.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"nucleolus","mod":"m3","n":"Nucleolus","d":"A dense region inside the nucleus where ribosomal RNA is made and ribosome subunits are put together. It has no membrane around it.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"nuclear-pores","mod":"m3","n":"Nuclear pores","d":"Protein-lined openings through the nuclear envelope. mRNA and ribosome subunits leave through them; proteins the nucleus needs come in through them.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"ribosome","mod":"m3","n":"Ribosome","d":"Makes proteins. Free ribosomes in the cytosol make proteins that stay in the cytosol. Ribosomes on the rough ER make proteins bound for membranes, for secretion, or for the endomembrane system.","p":"39","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"er","mod":"m3","n":"Endoplasmic reticulum","d":"A network of membrane sacs and tubes continuous with the nuclear envelope. Rough ER has ribosomes and makes membrane and secreted proteins. Smooth ER has no ribosomes and makes lipids and breaks down toxins.","p":"38–39","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"golgi","mod":"m3","n":"Golgi apparatus","d":"Stacks of flattened membrane sacs. Receives proteins and lipids from the ER, modifies them (for example by adding sugar chains), sorts them and ships them out in vesicles.","p":"39","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"lysosome","mod":"m3","n":"Lysosome","d":"A membrane sac full of digestive enzymes that work at acidic pH. Breaks down material brought in by endocytosis and worn-out parts of the cell.","p":"40","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"vesicle","mod":"m3","n":"Vesicle","d":"A small membrane sac that carries cargo between compartments, or to and from the plasma membrane.","p":"37–38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"endomembrane","mod":"m3","n":"Endomembrane system","d":"The connected set of membranes that make, modify and move proteins and lipids: nuclear envelope, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, vesicles and the plasma membrane. Parts are linked directly or by vesicles. Mitochondria and chloroplasts are not part of it.","p":"38","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"vacuole","mod":"m3","n":"Vacuole","d":"A membrane sac used for storage. Animal cells have small vacuoles. Plant cells usually have one large central vacuole that stores water and presses outward to keep the cell firm.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"mitochondria","mod":"m3","n":"Mitochondria","d":"Double-membrane organelles that make most of the cell's ATP by cellular respiration. The inner membrane folds into cristae, which adds surface area. They have their own DNA and ribosomes. Found in both animal and plant cells.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":0},{"k":"chloroplast","mod":"m3","n":"Chloroplast","d":"A double-membrane organelle in plants and algae where photosynthesis happens. Inside are stacks of thylakoids (grana). A chloroplast is one type of plastid. Animal cells do not have them.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"plastid","mod":"m3","n":"Plastid","d":"A family of plant organelles with a double membrane and their own DNA. Chloroplasts do photosynthesis, amyloplasts store starch, chromoplasts hold pigments. Not found in animal cells.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":null},{"k":"cell-wall","mod":"m3","n":"Cell wall","d":"A rigid layer outside the plasma membrane of plant cells, made mostly of cellulose. It resists swelling, so a plant cell in a hypotonic solution becomes firm instead of bursting. Animal cells do not have one.","p":"37","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":4},{"k":"diffusion","mod":"m3","n":"Diffusion","d":"Net movement of a substance from where it is more concentrated to where it is less concentrated, caused by random motion of molecules. It continues until the concentrations even out.","p":"43","g":"Passive transport","ch":5},{"k":"simple-diffusion","mod":"m3","n":"Simple diffusion","d":"Diffusion straight through the phospholipid bilayer, with no protein. Works for small nonpolar molecules such as O₂, CO₂ and steroid hormones.","p":"43","g":"Passive transport","ch":2},{"k":"facilitated-diffusion","mod":"m3","n":"Facilitated diffusion","d":"Diffusion through a membrane protein, either a channel or a carrier. Still passive: the substance moves down its gradient and no energy is spent.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":5},{"k":"passive-transport","mod":"m3","n":"Passive transport","d":"Movement across a membrane down a concentration or electrochemical gradient, with no energy spent by the cell. Includes simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion and osmosis.","p":"43","g":"Passive transport","ch":5},{"k":"channel","mod":"m3","n":"Channel protein","d":"A transmembrane protein that forms a water-filled pore. Specific ions or water flow through it quickly, down their gradient. Many channels are gated: they open or close in response to a signal.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":2},{"k":"carrier","mod":"m3","n":"Carrier protein","d":"A transmembrane protein that binds its cargo, changes shape, and releases it on the other side. Slower than a channel, and it can be saturated: once every carrier is busy, adding more cargo does not speed things up.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":2},{"k":"aquaporin","mod":"m3","n":"Aquaporin","d":"A channel protein for water. Most water that crosses a membrane goes through aquaporins.","p":"44","g":"Passive transport","ch":4},{"k":"concentration-gradient","mod":"m3","n":"Concentration gradient","d":"A difference in concentration of a substance between two places. Substances diffuse down their gradient (high to low) on their own; moving them up the gradient takes energy.","p":"43","g":"Active transport","ch":5},{"k":"osmosis","mod":"m3","n":"Osmosis","d":"Diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane. Water moves from the side with lower solute concentration (more free water) to the side with higher solute concentration.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"osmotic-pressure","mod":"m3","n":"Osmotic pressure","d":"The pressure that would be needed to stop water moving into a solution by osmosis. The more solute a solution has, the higher its osmotic pressure and the harder it pulls water in.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"isotonic","mod":"m3","n":"Isotonic","d":"The outside solution has the same solute concentration as the cell. Water moves both ways equally, so there is no net change in volume.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"hypotonic","mod":"m3","n":"Hypotonic","d":"The outside solution has a lower solute concentration than the cell. Water moves in. An animal cell swells and can burst (lysis).","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"hypertonic","mod":"m3","n":"Hypertonic","d":"The outside solution has a higher solute concentration than the cell. Water moves out. An animal cell shrinks and shrivels (crenation).","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"active-transport","mod":"m3","n":"Active transport","d":"Movement across a membrane against a gradient. It needs an energy source: ATP directly (primary) or another gradient (secondary).","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"primary-active","mod":"m3","n":"Primary active transport","d":"Active transport powered directly by ATP. The protein splits ATP and uses that energy to change shape and push cargo against its gradient. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump is the main example.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"nak-pump","mod":"m3","n":"Na⁺/K⁺ pump (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase)","d":"A transmembrane protein that uses ATP to move Na⁺ out of the cell and K⁺ into it, both against their gradients. It moves three Na⁺ out for every two K⁺ in, which also helps keep the inside of the cell negative. The Na⁺ gradient it builds powers secondary active transport.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"membrane-potential","mod":"m3","n":"Membrane potential","d":"The voltage across the plasma membrane. The inside of an animal cell is negative compared with the outside.","p":"47","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"electrochemical-gradient","mod":"m3","n":"Electrochemical gradient","d":"The total push on an ion across a membrane: its concentration gradient plus the pull from the charge difference across the membrane. For Na⁺, both push it into the cell. For K⁺, concentration pushes it out while the negative inside pulls it back.","p":"47","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"importer","mod":"m3","n":"Importer","d":"A transporter that moves its cargo into the cell. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump imports K⁺.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"exporter","mod":"m3","n":"Exporter","d":"A transporter that moves its cargo out of the cell. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump exports Na⁺.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"secondary-active","mod":"m3","n":"Secondary active transport","d":"Active transport powered by a gradient that a primary pump already built. One substance (usually Na⁺) flows down its gradient and that energy drags a second substance up its gradient. No ATP is used by this protein directly, but if the pump stops, this stops too.","p":"47","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"cotransport","mod":"m3","n":"Co-transport","d":"Two substances moved by the same protein at the same time. Symport and antiport are the two kinds.","p":"47–48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"uniporter","mod":"m3","n":"Uniporter","d":"A carrier that moves one kind of substance in one direction. The glucose transporter (GLUT) is a uniporter.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"symporter","mod":"m3","n":"Symporter","d":"A carrier that moves two substances in the same direction. The Na⁺/glucose symporter brings Na⁺ and glucose into the cell together.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"antiporter","mod":"m3","n":"Antiporter","d":"A carrier that moves two substances in opposite directions. The Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger brings Na⁺ in and sends H⁺ out.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"sglt","mod":"m3","n":"Na⁺/glucose symporter (SGLT)","d":"The apical carrier that brings glucose into gut lining cells against its gradient, powered by Na⁺ flowing in down its electrochemical gradient. Secondary active transport.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":7},{"k":"glut","mod":"m3","n":"Glucose transporter (GLUT)","d":"A uniporter carrier for glucose. On the basolateral side of a gut lining cell it lets glucose leave by facilitated diffusion, down its gradient into the interstitial fluid.","p":"48","g":"Co-transport","ch":5},{"k":"apical","mod":"m3","n":"Apical membrane","d":"The part of an epithelial cell's plasma membrane that faces the lumen (the gut contents). In gut lining cells it is folded into microvilli.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"basolateral","mod":"m3","n":"Basolateral membrane","d":"The sides and base of an epithelial cell, facing neighbouring cells and the interstitial fluid, toward the blood. It carries a different set of transport proteins from the apical side.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"tight-junction","mod":"m3","n":"Tight junction","d":"A seal between neighbouring epithelial cells near the apical surface. It stops gut contents leaking between cells and keeps apical and basolateral proteins from mixing.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"microvilli","mod":"m3","n":"Microvilli","d":"Finger-like folds of the apical membrane. They increase the surface area for absorption.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":null},{"k":"lumen","mod":"m3","n":"Gut lumen","d":"The space inside the small intestine, where digested food is.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"interstitial-fluid","mod":"m3","n":"Interstitial fluid","d":"The fluid that fills the space between cells in a tissue. Glucose leaving the basolateral side of a gut lining cell enters the interstitial fluid first, then diffuses into a capillary.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":0},{"k":"capillary","mod":"m3","n":"Capillary","d":"The smallest blood vessel. Its thin wall lets glucose, O₂ and CO₂ pass between blood and interstitial fluid.","p":"48","g":"The gut lining","ch":null},{"k":"atp","mod":"m3","n":"ATP","d":"Adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency. Splitting off a phosphate releases energy that pumps use. Most ATP is made in mitochondria.","p":"46","g":"Active transport","ch":6},{"k":"bulk-transport","mod":"m3","n":"Bulk transport","d":"Moving large particles, or a lot of material at once, into or out of the cell inside membrane vesicles. It needs energy.","p":"49","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"exocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Exocytosis","d":"A vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases its contents outside. It also adds the vesicle's membrane and proteins to the plasma membrane, which is how new transporters arrive.","p":"49","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"endocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Endocytosis","d":"The plasma membrane folds inward and pinches off a vesicle, bringing material in. Three kinds: phagocytosis, pinocytosis and receptor-mediated endocytosis.","p":"49","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"phagocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Phagocytosis","d":"Cell eating. The cell reaches around a large particle such as a bacterium with pseudopodia and engulfs it in a phagosome. The phagosome then fuses with a lysosome, which digests it.","p":"49–50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"pinocytosis","mod":"m3","n":"Pinocytosis","d":"Cell drinking. The cell takes in small droplets of extracellular fluid, with whatever is dissolved in it, in small vesicles. Not selective.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"rme","mod":"m3","n":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","d":"Selective endocytosis. Specific molecules (ligands) bind receptors clustered in coated pits. The pit, lined with clathrin on the inside, pinches off as a coated vesicle. Cells take in LDL (a cholesterol carrier) this way.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"amphipathic","mod":"m3","n":"Amphipathic","d":"Having both a hydrophilic (polar) region and a hydrophobic (nonpolar) region. Phospholipids are amphipathic: a polar head with glycerol, a phosphate and a polar group, and two nonpolar fatty acid tails.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"micelle","mod":"m3","n":"Micelle","d":"A small sphere of lipids with their hydrophobic tails buried inside and heads facing the water. Lipids with a large, bulky head and one tail form micelles. A micelle has no inner space.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"liposome","mod":"m3","n":"Liposome","d":"A closed, hollow bilayer that encloses an inner watery space. Phospholipids added to water form liposomes on their own, which is why a bilayer makes an effective cell membrane.","p":"34","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"lipid-raft","mod":"m3","n":"Lipid rafts","d":"Specialized microdomains of the membrane where certain lipids and proteins gather. They act as platforms for various cell processes.","p":"35","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"flip-flop","mod":"m3","n":"Lipid flip-flop","d":"A lipid moving from one layer of the bilayer to the other. It is rare (lipids move sideways easily instead), so the two layers can differ in composition.","p":"35","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"membrane-functions","mod":"m3","n":"Functions of membranes","d":"Compartmentalization, a selectively permeable barrier, transporting solutes, a scaffold for biochemical activities, responding to external signals, interaction between neighbouring cells, and energy transduction (converting one form of energy into another).","p":"33","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"membrane-protein-functions","mod":"m3","n":"Membrane protein functions","d":"Transporters move ions and molecules across; receptors let the cell receive signals; enzymes catalyze reactions; anchors attach to other proteins, such as the cytoskeleton.","p":"36","g":"Membrane structure","ch":3},{"k":"endosymbiosis","mod":"m3","n":"Endosymbiotic theory","d":"The idea that mitochondria and chloroplasts descend from free-living bacteria that were engulfed by an ancestral cell and kept. Evidence: two membranes, their own circular DNA and ribosomes, division by splitting, and bacterial size.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"outer-membrane","mod":"m3","n":"Double membrane (outer and inner)","d":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts are each surrounded by two membranes. In the endosymbiotic theory, the inner one came from the engulfed bacterium and the outer one from the host cell.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"cristae","mod":"m3","n":"Cristae","d":"The folds of the mitochondrial inner membrane. They increase the surface area where ATP is made.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"matrix","mod":"m3","n":"Mitochondrial matrix","d":"The space enclosed by the inner mitochondrial membrane. It holds the mitochondrion's DNA, ribosomes and many enzymes of cellular respiration.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"intermembrane-space","mod":"m3","n":"Intermembrane space","d":"The narrow space between the outer and inner mitochondrial membranes.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"stroma","mod":"m3","n":"Stroma","d":"The fluid inside the chloroplast's inner membrane, around the thylakoids. It holds the chloroplast's DNA and ribosomes, and is where sugars are built.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"thylakoid","mod":"m3","n":"Thylakoid","d":"A flattened membrane sac inside a chloroplast where light energy is captured. Thylakoids are stacked into grana.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"granum","mod":"m3","n":"Granum (plural grana)","d":"A stack of thylakoids in a chloroplast.","p":"42","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"mito-dna","mod":"m3","n":"Organelle DNA","d":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts carry their own small, circular DNA, like a bacterial chromosome, along with their own ribosomes.","p":"41","g":"Parts of the cell","ch":1},{"k":"crenation","mod":"m3","n":"Crenation","d":"An animal cell in a hypertonic solution loses water by osmosis, shrinks and shrivels. A red blood cell with a wrinkled, spiky outline is crenated.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"plasmolysis","mod":"m3","n":"Plasmolysis","d":"A plant cell in a hypertonic solution loses water, and its plasma membrane pulls away from the cell wall. The wall itself keeps its shape.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"lysis","mod":"m3","n":"Lysis (hemolysis)","d":"An animal cell in a hypotonic solution takes in water by osmosis, swells and can burst. When a red blood cell bursts this way it is called hemolysis.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"turgor","mod":"m3","n":"Turgor pressure","d":"The pressure of the cell contents pushing against the cell wall. A plant cell in a hypotonic solution takes in water and pushes against its wall; it becomes turgid (firm) instead of bursting.","p":"45","g":"Water and tonicity","ch":4},{"k":"endosome","mod":"m3","n":"Endosomes","d":"The network of tubules and vesicles that receives material brought in by receptor-mediated endocytosis. Early endosomes sit near the edge of the cell; late endosomes are deeper inside and also receive material from the Golgi.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"lysozyme","mod":"m3","n":"Lysozyme","d":"A lysosomal enzyme that breaks down bacterial cell walls. Together with the acidic pH of the lysosome and the H₂O₂ made by oxidases in the phagosome, it helps phagocytes such as macrophages and neutrophils kill microorganisms.","p":"50","g":"Bulk transport","ch":8},{"k":"dna","mod":"m4","n":"Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","d":"The molecule that stores genetic information in all organisms. A double-stranded polymer of deoxyribonucleotides. Its structure is tied to its two functions: storing genetic information and copying itself so that information passes from one generation to the next.","p":53,"g":"DNA structure","ch":0},{"k":"griffith","mod":"m4","n":"Griffith's experiment (1928)","d":"Frederick Griffith injected mice with two strains of Streptococcus pneumoniae. Heat-killed virulent bacteria alone did not kill mice, but mixed with live nonvirulent bacteria they did: something in the dead cells' debris turned nonvirulent cells virulent. The molecule was not identified at the time.","p":53,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"transformation","mod":"m4","n":"Transformation","d":"A change in a cell's traits caused by taking up genetic material from outside. In Griffith's experiment, nonvirulent bacteria became virulent after contact with debris from dead virulent cells.","p":54,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"avery","mod":"m4","n":"Avery, MacLeod and McCarty (1944)","d":"Extracted DNA, RNA and protein from heat-killed virulent bacteria and treated the extract with enzymes. Only DNase (which destroys DNA) stopped the extract from transforming nonvirulent bacteria. Key evidence that DNA is the genetic material.","p":54,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"nuclease","mod":"m4","n":"DNase, RNase and protease","d":"Enzymes that break down DNA, RNA and protein respectively. Avery's group used them to find out which molecule carried the transforming information.","p":54,"g":"The genetic material","ch":0},{"k":"nucleotide","mod":"m4","n":"Nucleotide","d":"The subunit of DNA and RNA. It has three parts: a five-carbon sugar, a nitrogenous base attached to the 1′ carbon, and one or more phosphate groups attached to the 5′ carbon. Memory trick: nucleoTide has Three parts.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"nucleoside","mod":"m4","n":"Nucleoside","d":"A base plus a sugar, with no phosphate. Memory trick: nucleoSide is just base and Sugar.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"deoxyribose","mod":"m4","n":"Deoxyribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in DNA. It has no oxygen at the 2′ carbon: just a hydrogen (–H) where ribose has a hydroxyl (–OH).","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"ribose","mod":"m4","n":"Ribose","d":"The five-carbon sugar in RNA. It has a hydroxyl (–OH) at the 2′ carbon. That extra –OH makes RNA less stable than DNA.","p":60,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"carbons","mod":"m4","n":"1′ to 5′ carbons","d":"The carbons of the sugar are numbered with primes (1′ to 5′) to tell them apart from atoms in the base. The base attaches at 1′, the phosphate at 5′, and the free –OH at 3′ is where the next nucleotide is added.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"purines","mod":"m4","n":"Purines","d":"Bases with a double ring: adenine (A) and guanine (G).","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"pyrimidines","mod":"m4","n":"Pyrimidines","d":"Bases with a single ring: cytosine (C), thymine (T, DNA only) and uracil (U, RNA only).","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"adenine","mod":"m4","n":"Adenine (A)","d":"A purine. Pairs with thymine in DNA (two hydrogen bonds) and with uracil in RNA.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"guanine","mod":"m4","n":"Guanine (G)","d":"A purine. Pairs with cytosine through three hydrogen bonds.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"thymine","mod":"m4","n":"Thymine (T)","d":"A pyrimidine found only in DNA. It has a methyl (–CH₃) group where uracil has a hydrogen. Pairs with adenine.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"cytosine","mod":"m4","n":"Cytosine (C)","d":"A pyrimidine found in DNA and RNA. Pairs with guanine through three hydrogen bonds.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":null},{"k":"uracil","mod":"m4","n":"Uracil (U)","d":"The pyrimidine that replaces thymine in RNA. It has a hydrogen where thymine has a methyl group. In RNA, A pairs with U.","p":60,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"phosphate","mod":"m4","n":"Phosphate group","d":"Attached to the 5′ carbon of the sugar. At cellular pH (about 7) its hydroxyl groups are ionized, giving it negative charges. Sugars and phosphates together form the backbone of a strand.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"phosphodiester","mod":"m4","n":"Phosphodiester bond","d":"The link between nucleotides in a strand: a phosphate joins the 3′ carbon of one sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next. The chain of sugars and phosphates is the backbone.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"polarity","mod":"m4","n":"Polarity (5′ and 3′ ends)","d":"The two ends of a strand differ. One has a free 5′ phosphate, the other a free 3′ hydroxyl. Sequences are written 5′ to 3′; if the ends are not labelled, the left end is 5′.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"five-three","mod":"m4","n":"5′→3′ vs. 3′→5′","d":"Bases can only be added to the 3′–OH of the sugar, so every nucleic acid grows 5′→3′. The template strand is read in the opposite direction, 3′→5′.","p":55,"g":"Building blocks","ch":1},{"k":"double-helix","mod":"m4","n":"Double helix","d":"The 3-D shape of DNA: two strands wrapped around each other in a right-handed helix, sugar-phosphate backbones on the outside and bases pointing in. About 10 base pairs (3.4 nm) per turn and 2 nm across.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"watson-crick","mod":"m4","n":"Watson, Crick, Franklin and Wilkins","d":"James Watson and Francis Crick built the 3-D model of DNA using Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins's X-ray crystallography and Erwin Chargaff's base composition data.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"chargaff","mod":"m4","n":"Chargaff's rule","d":"In DNA, %A = %T and %C = %G. This is because A always pairs with T, and C with G.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"antiparallel","mod":"m4","n":"Antiparallel","d":"The two strands of a double helix run in opposite directions: one 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"complementary","mod":"m4","n":"Complementary","d":"Bases that pair with each other: A with T, and G with C. Knowing one strand tells you the other. (Complementary means completing each other. Complimentary means free.)","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"grooves","mod":"m4","n":"Major and minor grooves","d":"The uneven pair of grooves on the outside of the helix, formed because the two backbones are not evenly spaced around it.","p":56,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"hydrogen-bonding","mod":"m4","n":"Hydrogen bonding","d":"Weak bonds between paired bases: two between A and T, three between G and C. Weak alone, strong in large numbers. Because they are weak, DNA can unzip down the middle.","p":57,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"base-stacking","mod":"m4","n":"Base stacking","d":"Interactions between neighbouring bases on the same strand, stacked on top of each other. Together with hydrogen bonds, it keeps the double helix stable.","p":57,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"chromatin","mod":"m4","n":"Chromatin and condensation","d":"DNA wraps around histone proteins (H2A, H2B, H3, H4) to form nucleosomes (a 10 nm fibre), which coil into a 30 nm fibre, then 300 nm and 700 nm coils, and finally a condensed chromatid about 1400 nm across.","p":53,"g":"DNA structure","ch":2},{"k":"replication","mod":"m4","n":"Replication","d":"Copying DNA so it can pass to the next generation. This is one of DNA's two functions. In this module it is only mentioned: complementary base pairing lets each strand act as a guide for a new partner strand.","p":53,"g":"DNA structure","ch":0},{"k":"genome","mod":"m4","n":"Genome","d":"The genetic material of an organism, or of a compartment: the bacterial genome, the nuclear genome, the mitochondrial genome.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"gene","mod":"m4","n":"Gene","d":"The unit of heredity affecting one or more traits of an organism; the DNA sequence that corresponds to a specific protein.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"gene-expression","mod":"m4","n":"Gene expression","d":"The production of a functional gene product, such as a protein. Genes can be expressed (turned on) or not expressed (turned off), and expression is regulated.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"central-dogma","mod":"m4","n":"Central dogma","d":"DNA is transcribed into RNA, which is then translated into protein. Exception: reverse transcription makes DNA from an RNA template.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"transcription","mod":"m4","n":"Transcription","d":"Making RNA from a DNA template. The name stresses that DNA and RNA use the same language of nucleic acids. Three stages: initiation, elongation and termination.","p":58,"g":"Transcription","ch":3},{"k":"translation","mod":"m4","n":"Translation","d":"Making protein from the code on an mRNA, carried out by ribosomes. The name signals a change of language, from nucleic acids to amino acids. Covered in detail in a later module.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"ribosome","mod":"m4","n":"Ribosomes","d":"The machines that translate mRNA into protein. Always in the cytoplasm. In prokaryotes they start on an mRNA before its transcription is even finished.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"reverse-transcription","mod":"m4","n":"Reverse transcription","d":"Making DNA from an RNA template, catalyzed by reverse transcriptase (an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase). HIV uses it to convert its RNA genome into DNA.","p":58,"g":"Gene expression","ch":3},{"k":"rna","mod":"m4","n":"Ribonucleic acid (RNA)","d":"A polymer of ribonucleotides. Like DNA it has polarity, but it uses ribose and uracil, has a triphosphate at its 5′ end, is usually shorter and single-stranded, and can fold into complex shapes.","p":60,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"mrna","mod":"m4","n":"Messenger RNA (mRNA)","d":"The RNA that carries a gene's code to the ribosome for protein synthesis.","p":58,"g":"RNA","ch":4},{"k":"rna-polymerase","mod":"m4","n":"RNA polymerase","d":"The enzyme that carries out transcription. It unwinds DNA, reads the template strand 3′→5′ and builds RNA 5′→3′. Prokaryotes have one type. Eukaryotes have three; RNA Pol II makes mRNA.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"template","mod":"m4","n":"Template vs nontemplate strand","d":"The template strand is the one RNA polymerase reads; the RNA is complementary to it. The nontemplate strand has the same sequence as the RNA, with T instead of U. Which strand is the template can differ from gene to gene.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"ntp","mod":"m4","n":"Ribonucleoside triphosphates","d":"The building blocks for RNA: ATP, GTP, CTP and UTP. Their extra phosphates provide the energy for the synthetic reaction. Two phosphates are released as pyrophosphate each time one is added.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"pyrophosphate","mod":"m4","n":"Pyrophosphate (P–P)","d":"The two phosphates released when a ribonucleotide is added. The 3′–OH of the growing strand attacks the innermost phosphate of the incoming triphosphate, and the other two leave as pyrophosphate.","p":64,"g":"Transcription","ch":6},{"k":"rna-transcript","mod":"m4","n":"RNA transcript","d":"The RNA strand made during transcription. It is complementary to the template strand and grows in the 5′→3′ direction.","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":6},{"k":"stages","mod":"m4","n":"Initiation, elongation, termination","d":"The three stages of transcription: RNA polymerase binds at the promoter and opens the DNA (initiation), adds nucleotides one at a time (elongation), and stops and releases the RNA at a terminator (termination).","p":61,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"promoter","mod":"m4","n":"Promoter","d":"A conserved double-stranded DNA sequence where transcription factors and RNA polymerase bind, marking where transcription starts.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"tata","mod":"m4","n":"TATA box","d":"A very common promoter sequence in eukaryotes, 5′-TATAAA-3′. The first nucleotide to be transcribed is usually about 25 base pairs away from it.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"terminator","mod":"m4","n":"Terminator","d":"The DNA sequence where transcription ends. RNA polymerase transcribes until it reaches one.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"sigma","mod":"m4","n":"Sigma factor","d":"The bacterial protein that recognizes promoters. It associates with RNA polymerase and directs it to bind specific promoters. Eukaryotes do not use it.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"gtf","mod":"m4","n":"General transcription factors","d":"In eukaryotes, at least six proteins that work together to start transcription. They bind the promoter region and help position RNA Pol II.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"activator","mod":"m4","n":"Transcriptional activator protein","d":"A protein that binds an enhancer. Through looping of the DNA, it contacts the proteins at the promoter and helps start transcription.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"enhancer","mod":"m4","n":"Enhancer","d":"A DNA sequence, often far from the promoter, that transcriptional activator proteins bind.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"mediator","mod":"m4","n":"Mediator complex","d":"A large protein complex that associates with the general transcription factors and RNA Pol II, and bridges them to activator proteins when the DNA loops.","p":63,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"pol2","mod":"m4","n":"RNA polymerase II","d":"The eukaryotic RNA polymerase that makes mRNA. One of three eukaryotic RNA polymerases.","p":62,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"bubble","mod":"m4","n":"Transcription bubble","d":"The unwound stretch of DNA inside RNA polymerase, about 14 base pairs long. Inside it, about 8 base pairs of the new RNA are paired with the template strand.","p":63,"g":"Transcription","ch":5},{"k":"channels","mod":"m4","n":"Polymerase channels","d":"RNA polymerase has channels for incoming nucleotides, for the DNA double helix to enter and exit, for the growing RNA to exit, and for releasing the RNA when transcription ends.","p":63,"g":"Transcription","ch":6},{"k":"primary-transcript","mod":"m4","n":"Primary transcript","d":"The RNA that comes straight off the template strand. In prokaryotes it is already the mRNA. In eukaryotes it (pre-mRNA) must be processed first.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":7},{"k":"polycistronic","mod":"m4","n":"Polycistronic mRNA","d":"A prokaryotic mRNA that carries the information for more than one gene.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":7},{"k":"coupled","mod":"m4","n":"Coupled transcription and translation","d":"In prokaryotes there is no nuclear envelope, so ribosomes start translating an mRNA before transcription is finished.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":7},{"k":"rna-processing","mod":"m4","n":"RNA processing","d":"The chemical changes that turn a eukaryotic primary transcript into mRNA that can leave the nucleus: adding a 5′ cap, removing introns, and adding a poly(A) tail.","p":65,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"cap","mod":"m4","n":"5′ cap","d":"A 7-methylguanosine added to the 5′ end of the primary transcript by a unique 5′–5′ bond through three phosphates. It protects the mRNA from exonucleases and is needed for the ribosome to recognize the mRNA.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"polya","mod":"m4","n":"Polyadenylation: poly(A) tail","d":"Adding about 250 adenines to the 3′ end of the mRNA. The tail has roles in transcription termination, export of the mRNA to the cytoplasm, and protection from exonucleases.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"exonuclease","mod":"m4","n":"Exonucleases","d":"Enzymes that break down nucleic acids from their ends. The 5′ cap and poly(A) tail protect mRNA from them.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"splicing","mod":"m4","n":"RNA splicing","d":"Cutting introns out of the primary transcript and joining the exons together.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"introns-exons","mod":"m4","n":"Introns and exons","d":"Introns are sequences removed from the primary transcript. Exons are the sequences left intact and joined together in the mRNA.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8},{"k":"alt-splicing","mod":"m4","n":"Alternative splicing","d":"Splicing one gene's primary transcript in different ways, so a single gene can produce different but related proteins in different cells.","p":66,"g":"RNA processing","ch":8}],"exam":{"m1":[{"ch":0,"p":"4","q":"Who first described and named \"cells\", and what was he looking at?","opts":[["Robert Hooke, looking at cork",1,"In 1665 he saw the empty walls of dead cork cells."],["Anton van Leeuwenhoek, looking at pond water",0,"Leeuwenhoek saw living animalcules, but Hooke named cells first."],["Schleiden, looking at plant embryos",0,"Schleiden came much later, with the cell theory."],["Virchow, looking at blood",0,"Virchow added the third tenet of the cell theory."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"4","q":"What did Leeuwenhoek see that Hooke did not?","opts":[["Living, moving single-celled organisms",1,"He called them animalcules."],["Cell walls",0,"Hooke saw walls; Leeuwenhoek saw living cells."],["Viruses",0,"Viruses are far too small for a light microscope."],["Mitochondria",0,"Early lenses could not show organelles."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"4","q":"Which kind of microscopy uses dyes such as DAPI to make specific structures glow?","opts":[["Fluorescence microscopy",1,"DAPI labels the nucleus; phalloidin labels actin."],["Light microscopy with no stain",0,"Plain light microscopy does not make structures glow."],["Electron microscopy",0,"Electron microscopes use electron beams, not glowing dyes."],["Hooke's microscope",0,"Hooke used simple light microscopy."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"5","q":"Which list gives the three tenets of the cell theory?","opts":[["All organisms are made of one or more cells; the cell is the structural and functional unit of life; cells arise only from pre-existing cells",1,""],["All cells have a nucleus; all cells have mitochondria; all cells have a cell wall",0,"Prokaryotes have no nucleus, and animal cells have no wall."],["Cells come from nonliving matter; cells are alive; cells divide",0,"The cell theory rejects spontaneous generation."],["DNA makes RNA; RNA makes protein; protein makes cells",0,"That's closer to the central dogma."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"5","q":"Who proposed the first two tenets of the cell theory?","opts":[["Schleiden and Schwann",1,"A botanist and a zoologist."],["Hooke and Leeuwenhoek",0,"They made the first observations, not the theory."],["Virchow alone",0,"Virchow added the third tenet."],["Watson and Crick",0,"They described DNA's structure."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"5","q":"Virchow was a ___ who added the idea that ___.","opts":[["physician; cells arise only from pre-existing cells",1,""],["botanist; plant tissues are made of cells",0,"That was Schleiden."],["zoologist; animals are made of cells",0,"That was Schwann."],["microscopist; cork is made of cells",0,"That was Hooke."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"5","q":"Which is true of viruses?","opts":[["They are inert outside a living cell and need a host cell to reproduce",1,""],["They have their own ribosomes",0,"Viruses use the host's ribosomes."],["They have no nucleic acid",0,"That describes prions."],["They reproduce by binary fission",0,"That is how prokaryotes reproduce."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"5","q":"A small circular RNA with no protein coat that infects plants is a:","opts":[["Viroid",1,"Example: potato spindle tuber viroid."],["Virus",0,"Viruses have a protein coat."],["Prion",0,"Prions are protein with no nucleic acid."],["Plasmid",0,"Plasmids are DNA circles inside bacteria."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"5","q":"How does a prion cause disease?","opts":[["A misfolded protein makes normal copies of the protein misfold, and the misfolded form accumulates",1,"PrP^Sc converts PrP^C."],["It inserts its DNA into the host genome",0,"Prions have no DNA."],["It reproduces using the host's ribosomes",0,"No nucleic acid, no genes to translate."],["It digests the cell wall",0,"Not how prions work."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What is the difference between cytoplasm and cytosol?","opts":[["Cytoplasm is all the contents inside the membrane; cytosol is the fluid part, cytoplasm minus organelles",1,""],["They mean exactly the same thing",0,"Cytosol excludes the organelles."],["Cytosol includes the organelles; cytoplasm doesn't",0,"It's the other way around."],["Cytoplasm is only found in prokaryotes",0,"All cells have cytoplasm."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What is a gene?","opts":[["The DNA code for the information needed to produce a functional product, such as a protein",1,""],["A protein that copies DNA",0,"That's an enzyme, not a gene."],["All the DNA in a cell",0,"That would be the genome."],["A molecule of ATP",0,"ATP is an energy carrier."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"In the central dogma, making RNA from DNA is called ___ and making protein from RNA is called ___.","opts":[["transcription; translation",1,""],["translation; transcription",0,"Reversed."],["replication; transcription",0,"Replication copies DNA into DNA."],["transcription; replication",0,"Protein is made by translation."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"ATP is made of:","opts":[["Adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups",1,"With two phosphates it's ADP; with one, AMP."],["Adenine, deoxyribose and one phosphate",0,"ATP has ribose and three phosphates."],["Three amino acids",0,"ATP is a nucleotide, not a peptide."],["Glucose and three phosphates",0,"The sugar is ribose and there is a base, adenine."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"6","q":"A cell loses the ability to control its own division and grows uncontrollably. Which basic property has failed?","opts":[["Self-regulation",1,""],["Acquiring energy",0,"Energy use isn't the problem here."],["Having a membrane",0,"The membrane is still there."],["Producing more of themselves",0,"It's producing too many, because regulation failed."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"Which describes prokaryotes?","opts":[["No nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles; a single circular chromosome",1,""],["A nucleus but no mitochondria",0,"Prokaryotes have no nucleus."],["Always multicellular",0,"Prokaryotes are single cells."],["Found only in extreme environments",0,"Some archaea are, but prokaryotes are everywhere: the most abundant organisms on Earth."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"What are the three domains of life?","opts":[["Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya",1,""],["Plants, animals and fungi",0,"Those are groups within Eukarya."],["Prokaryotes, viruses and eukaryotes",0,"Viruses aren't on the tree of cellular life."],["Protists, bacteria and archaea",0,"Protists are eukaryotes."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"Which feature does your notes give for Archaea but not Bacteria?","opts":[["An isoprenoid cell membrane",1,"They often live in extreme environments too."],["A single circular chromosome",0,"Both have one."],["No nucleus",0,"Both lack a nucleus."],["Ribosomes",0,"All cells have ribosomes."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"7","q":"How do prokaryotes reproduce?","opts":[["Binary fission",1,""],["Budding",0,"Yeast buds."],["Mitosis inside a nucleus",0,"There is no nucleus."],["Only inside a host cell",0,"That describes viruses."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"8","q":"Fungal cell walls contain:","opts":[["Chitin, a modified polysaccharide",1,""],["Cellulose and chloroplasts",0,"Fungi have no chloroplasts."],["Cholesterol",0,"Cholesterol is in animal membranes."],["Nothing; fungi have no cell walls",0,"They do have walls."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"9","q":"Which set of features belongs to plant cells but not animal cells?","opts":[["Cell wall, large water-filled vacuole, chloroplasts, plasmodesmata",1,""],["Nucleus, mitochondria, ER",0,"Animal cells have these too."],["No cell wall and a distinct mode of early development",0,"Those describe animals."],["Nucleoid and plasmids",0,"Those are prokaryote features."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"8–9","q":"An organism that depends on preformed organic molecules for its carbon and energy is a:","opts":[["Heterotroph",1,"Fungi and animals are heterotrophs."],["Autotroph",0,"Autotrophs make their own food."],["Prokaryote",0,"That describes cell type, not how it feeds."],["Viroid",0,"Viroids don't feed at all."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"8","q":"Volvox, slime molds, water molds and ciliates are all:","opts":[["Protists",1,"A diverse group, mostly single cells."],["Fungi",0,"Slime and water molds are protists, despite the names."],["Bacteria",0,"They are eukaryotes."],["Plants",0,"Only Volvox does photosynthesis, and it's still classed as a protist (alga)."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"9","q":"Which of these is NOT part of the endomembrane system?","opts":[["Mitochondria",1,"Mitochondria, chloroplasts, peroxisomes and ribosomes are outside it."],["Golgi apparatus",0,"Part of the EMS."],["Nuclear envelope",0,"Part of the EMS."],["Lysosomes",0,"Part of the EMS."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"9","q":"Which organelle modifies and sorts proteins and lipids on their way to their destinations?","opts":[["Golgi apparatus",1,""],["Lysosome",0,"Lysosomes break things down."],["Nucleolus",0,"The nucleolus makes ribosome parts."],["Peroxisome",0,"Peroxisomes break down fatty acids."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What does the cytoskeleton do?","opts":[["Provides an internal structural framework of protein filaments",1,""],["Makes ATP",0,"That's mitochondria."],["Stores DNA",0,"That's the nucleus."],["Breaks down macromolecules",0,"That's lysosomes."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"10","q":"Which model organism is the \"minimal model eukaryote\" used to study the cell cycle?","opts":[["Saccharomyces cerevisiae (yeast)",1,""],["Escherichia coli",0,"E. coli is a prokaryote."],["Mus musculus",0,"The mouse is the model mammal."],["Arabidopsis thaliana",0,"Arabidopsis is the plant model."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"10","q":"E. coli is used as a model organism mainly to study:","opts":[["DNA replication, transcription and translation",1,""],["Flowering",0,"That's Arabidopsis."],["Embryo development in insects",0,"That's Drosophila."],["The fate of every cell in an animal",0,"That's C. elegans."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"7–9","q":"You find a cell with a cell wall, a nucleus, mitochondria and no chloroplasts. Its wall contains chitin. What is it most likely?","opts":[["A fungal cell",1,"Chitin walls and no chloroplasts: a fungus such as yeast."],["A plant cell",0,"Plant walls are polysaccharide without chitin, and leaf cells have chloroplasts."],["A bacterium",0,"Bacteria have no nucleus or mitochondria."],["An animal cell",0,"Animal cells have no wall."]]}],"m2":[{"ch":0,"p":"13","q":"What is in the nucleus of an atom?","opts":[["Protons and neutrons",1,"Electrons move around the nucleus."],["Protons and electrons",0,"Electrons are outside the nucleus."],["Only electrons",0,"Electrons orbit; they aren't in the nucleus."],["Neutrons and electrons",0,"Protons are in the nucleus too, and electrons are outside."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"13","q":"Valence electrons are:","opts":[["The electrons in the outermost orbitals, which decide how many bonds an atom makes",1,""],["The electrons closest to the nucleus",0,"Those are inner electrons."],["Electrons that have left the atom",0,"An atom that loses electrons becomes an ion."],["Another name for protons",0,"Protons are in the nucleus."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"13","q":"How many electrons can one orbital hold?","opts":[["2",1,""],["8",0,"Eight fills a whole shell of several orbitals."],["4",0,"Too many for one orbital."],["1",0,"An orbital can hold a pair."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"14","q":"In a water molecule, oxygen pulls the shared electrons closer than hydrogen does. This makes the O–H bond:","opts":[["Polar covalent",1,"Oxygen is δ⁻ and each hydrogen δ⁺."],["Nonpolar covalent",0,"That needs equal sharing."],["Ionic",0,"No electron is fully transferred."],["A hydrogen bond",0,"Hydrogen bonds form between molecules."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"14","q":"Electronegativity is:","opts":[["How strongly an atom attracts shared electrons",1,""],["The number of electrons an atom has",0,"That's set by the protons in a neutral atom."],["The charge of an ion",0,"Charge comes from gaining or losing electrons."],["The mass of the nucleus",0,"Unrelated."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"15","q":"An atom that has one more proton than electrons is a:","opts":[["Cation",1,"It is positively charged, like Na⁺."],["Anion",0,"An anion has an extra electron."],["Neutral atom",0,"Neutral atoms have equal numbers."],["Isotope",0,"Not a term in this module."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"14–15","q":"Which bond involves one atom taking an electron from another?","opts":[["Ionic bond",1,"As in NaCl: Na⁺ and Cl⁻."],["Nonpolar covalent bond",0,"Electrons are shared equally."],["Polar covalent bond",0,"Electrons are shared unequally, not taken."],["Hydrogen bond",0,"A weak attraction between molecules."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"15","q":"When NaCl dissolves in water, the δ⁺ hydrogens of water face:","opts":[["The Cl⁻ ions",1,"Opposite charges attract. The δ⁻ oxygens face Na⁺."],["The Na⁺ ions",0,"Like charges would repel."],["Each other",0,"They face the ions."],["Nothing; water ignores ions",0,"Water surrounds the ions, which is why salt dissolves."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"16","q":"A hydrogen bond is:","opts":[["A weak bond between an electronegative atom and a hydrogen already covalently bonded to another atom",1,""],["A strong covalent bond between two hydrogens",0,"That's the bond in H₂."],["An ionic bond involving H⁺",0,"Not how the notes define it."],["The bond inside a water molecule",0,"That's polar covalent."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"16","q":"Oil and water don't mix because oil is:","opts":[["Nonpolar, so it is hydrophobic",1,"Water hydrogen-bonds to itself and pushes oil together."],["Polar, so it is hydrophilic",0,"Hydrophilic things dissolve in water."],["Ionic",0,"Oils are hydrocarbons."],["Too acidic",0,"pH isn't the reason."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"16","q":"In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, what are H₂ and O₂?","opts":[["Reactants",1,"The starting substances."],["Products",0,"Water is the product."],["Catalysts",0,"They are used up."],["Isomers",0,"Different molecules, not isomers."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"17","q":"A solution with a pH of 3 is:","opts":[["Acidic, with more H⁺ than pure water",1,""],["Basic, with fewer H⁺",0,"Basic is above 7."],["Neutral",0,"Neutral is 7."],["Basic, with more H⁺",0,"More H⁺ means acidic."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"17","q":"Solution A is pH 4 and solution B is pH 6. Compared with B, A has:","opts":[["100 times more H⁺",1,"Two pH units: 10 × 10."],["2 times more H⁺",0,"pH is a log scale."],["100 times less H⁺",0,"Lower pH means more H⁺."],["The same amount of H⁺",0,"Different pH means different H⁺."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"18","q":"Why is carbon the backbone of organic molecules?","opts":[["It forms four covalent bonds and can build chains and rings",1,""],["It forms ionic bonds with water",0,"Carbon shares electrons."],["It has one valence electron",0,"It has four."],["It is the lightest element",0,"Hydrogen is."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"20","q":"Which functional group is nonpolar?","opts":[["Methyl, –CH₃",1,""],["Hydroxyl, –OH",0,"Polar."],["Carboxyl, –COOH",0,"Polar and acidic."],["Amino, –NH₂",0,"Polar and basic."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"20","q":"Which group behaves as an acid and is negatively charged at the pH of a cell?","opts":[["Carboxyl",1,""],["Amino",0,"Amino is positive and acts as a base."],["Methyl",0,"Methyl is nonpolar and uncharged."],["Hydroxyl",0,"Hydroxyl is polar but uncharged."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"19","q":"A condensation reaction:","opts":[["Links monomers with a covalent bond and releases water",1,"It requires energy."],["Uses water to break a polymer apart",0,"That's hydrolysis."],["Only happens in lipids",0,"It builds proteins, nucleic acids and polysaccharides."],["Releases energy as it breaks bonds",0,"Hydrolysis releases energy."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"19","q":"How many water molecules are used to completely hydrolyze a chain of 8 glucose units?","opts":[["7",1,"One per bond, and 8 units have 7 bonds."],["8",0,"Count the bonds."],["9",0,"More than the bonds."],["16",0,"One water per bond."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"19, 29","q":"Which macromolecule is not a polymer?","opts":[["Lipids",1,"They are grouped together because they are hydrophobic."],["Proteins",0,"Polymers of amino acids."],["Nucleic acids",0,"Polymers of nucleotides."],["Polysaccharides",0,"Polymers of monosaccharides."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"21","q":"What distinguishes one amino acid from another?","opts":[["Its R group (side chain)",1,""],["Its α carbon",0,"All amino acids have one."],["Its carboxyl group",0,"Shared by all."],["Its amino group",0,"Shared by all."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"22","q":"A peptide bond forms between:","opts":[["The carboxyl carbon of one amino acid and the amino nitrogen of the next",1,"Water is released."],["Two R groups",0,"R groups are side chains, not the backbone link."],["Two amino groups",0,"One carboxyl and one amino group are needed."],["A sugar and a phosphate",0,"That's in nucleic acids."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"21","q":"Antibodies are an example of which protein function?","opts":[["Defense",1,"Along with complement proteins."],["Transport",0,"Transport proteins move molecules."],["Support",0,"Support proteins include the cytoskeleton."],["Movement",0,"Motor and contractile proteins do that."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"23–24","q":"A nucleotide is made of:","opts":[["A nitrogenous base, a five-carbon sugar and one or more phosphate groups",1,"NucleoTide has Three parts."],["A base and a sugar only",0,"That's a nucleoside."],["An amino acid and a sugar",0,"Amino acids build proteins."],["Glycerol and fatty acids",0,"That's a lipid."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"23","q":"Which bases are purines?","opts":[["Adenine and guanine (two rings)",1,""],["Cytosine, thymine and uracil",0,"Those are pyrimidines, with one ring."],["Thymine and uracil only",0,"Pyrimidines."],["All five bases",0,"Only A and G are purines."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"23–24","q":"How does RNA differ from DNA?","opts":[["RNA has ribose (–OH on the 2′ carbon) and uracil instead of thymine",1,""],["RNA has deoxyribose and thymine",0,"That's DNA."],["RNA has no phosphate groups",0,"Both have phosphates."],["RNA has no bases",0,"Both have bases."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"25","q":"New nucleotides are added to a growing strand at its:","opts":[["3′ end (the free 3′–OH)",1,"Chains are built 5′ → 3′."],["5′ end",0,"The chain begins at the 5′ end."],["Middle",0,"Only at the 3′ end."],["Either end",0,"Only the 3′ end."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"24","q":"cAMP, ATP and NAD show that nucleotides can also act as:","opts":[["Signal molecules, energy carriers and coenzymes",1,""],["Cell wall components",0,"Not a nucleotide job."],["Hormones only",0,"Too narrow."],["Membrane lipids",0,"That's phospholipids."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"26","q":"Glucose, galactose and fructose all have the formula C₆H₁₂O₆. They are:","opts":[["Isomers: same formula, different arrangement",1,""],["Identical molecules",0,"They differ in configuration and function."],["Polysaccharides",0,"They are monosaccharides."],["Lipids",0,"They are carbohydrates."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"27–28","q":"Sucrose is:","opts":[["A disaccharide of glucose and fructose, joined by a glycosidic bond",1,"Table sugar, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁."],["A polysaccharide of many glucose units",0,"That's starch or glycogen."],["A monosaccharide",0,"It is two sugars."],["A disaccharide of two glucose units",0,"One of them is fructose."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"28","q":"Which polysaccharide gives structural support?","opts":[["Cellulose",1,""],["Starch",0,"Energy storage in plants."],["Glycogen",0,"Energy storage."],["Sucrose",0,"A disaccharide."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"29","q":"A triacylglycerol is made of:","opts":[["Glycerol and three fatty acids",1,"Fats and oils, for energy storage."],["Glycerol, a phosphate and two fatty acids",0,"That's a phospholipid."],["Four fused rings",0,"That's a steroid."],["Three glucose units",0,"That's a carbohydrate."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"29","q":"An unsaturated fatty acid has:","opts":[["One or more C=C double bonds, which kink the chain",1,""],["No double bonds",0,"That's saturated."],["A phosphate head",0,"That's a phospholipid."],["Four rings",0,"That's a steroid."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"30","q":"Why are phospholipids described as amphipathic?","opts":[["They have a hydrophilic phosphate head and two hydrophobic fatty acid tails",1,""],["They are entirely hydrophobic",0,"The head is polar."],["They are polymers",0,"Lipids aren't polymers."],["They carry genetic information",0,"That's nucleic acids."]]},{"ch":9,"p":"30","q":"Cholesterol is:","opts":[["A steroid found in animal cell membranes and a precursor of steroid hormones",1,"For example cortisol."],["A fatty acid",0,"It has four fused rings."],["A polysaccharide",0,"It is a lipid."],["A protein",0,"It is a lipid."]]}],"m3":[{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"Which of these is part of the endomembrane system?","opts":[["Golgi apparatus",1,"The endomembrane system is the nuclear envelope, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, vesicles and plasma membrane."],["Mitochondrion",0,"Mitochondria are not connected to the endomembrane system."],["Chloroplast",0,"Chloroplasts are separate, like mitochondria."],["Ribosome",0,"Ribosomes have no membrane."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"A protein will be secreted from the cell. Where is it made?","opts":[["On ribosomes attached to the rough ER",1,"Membrane and secreted proteins start on the rough ER, then go to the Golgi."],["On free ribosomes in the cytosol",0,"Those make proteins that stay in the cytosol."],["In the nucleolus",0,"The nucleolus makes ribosome parts."],["In the smooth ER",0,"Smooth ER makes lipids and breaks down toxins."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"What is the main job of the smooth ER?","opts":[["Making lipids and breaking down toxins",1,""],["Making proteins for secretion",0,"That's the rough ER."],["Digesting worn-out organelles",0,"That's lysosomes."],["Making ATP",0,"That's mitochondria."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"37–40","q":"What passes through nuclear pores?","opts":[["mRNA and ribosome subunits going out, and proteins the nucleus needs coming in",1,""],["Only water",0,"Pores move large molecules too."],["Vesicles from the Golgi",0,"Vesicles don't pass through nuclear pores."],["Nothing; the nuclear envelope is sealed",0,"The pores connect the nucleus and cytoplasm."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"Which observation supports the endosymbiotic theory?","opts":[["Mitochondria have their own circular DNA and ribosomes, like bacteria",1,"They also have a double membrane and divide by splitting, like bacteria."],["Mitochondria are part of the endomembrane system",0,"They aren't; that's part of why they're thought to have a separate origin."],["Mitochondria are found only in plants",0,"They're in nearly all eukaryotes."],["Mitochondria are made by the Golgi",0,"Mitochondria come only from other mitochondria."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"Why do mitochondria and chloroplasts have two membranes, according to the endosymbiotic theory?","opts":[["The inner one came from the engulfed bacterium and the outer one from the host cell that engulfed it",1,""],["One membrane is for lipids and one for proteins",0,"Not the explanation."],["They were made by fusing two vesicles",0,"Not the explanation."],["All organelles have two membranes",0,"Most organelles have one."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"Where in the mitochondrion is the inner membrane folded to add surface area?","opts":[["The cristae",1,"The folds raise the surface area for making ATP."],["The stroma",0,"The stroma is the fluid inside a chloroplast."],["The thylakoids",0,"Those are in chloroplasts."],["The nucleolus",0,"That's in the nucleus."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"In a chloroplast, stacks of thylakoids are called:","opts":[["Grana",1,"The fluid around them is the stroma."],["Cristae",0,"Cristae are folds in mitochondria."],["Cisternae",0,"Those are Golgi sacs."],["Microvilli",0,"Those are on intestinal cells."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"33–34, 36","q":"An integral membrane protein differs from a peripheral one because it:","opts":[["Is held in the bilayer by hydrophobic regions and can't be removed without breaking the membrane",1,""],["Only sits on the outer surface",0,"Peripheral proteins sit on surfaces."],["Has no hydrophobic parts",0,"Integral proteins have hydrophobic parts among the tails."],["Is always a glycoprotein",0,"Not always."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"33–34, 36","q":"Which molecule crosses a pure phospholipid bilayer most easily?","opts":[["O₂",1,"Small and nonpolar."],["Na⁺",0,"Charged ions can't enter the hydrophobic core."],["Glucose",0,"Too big and polar."],["A protein",0,"Far too big."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"33–34, 36","q":"In the fluid mosaic model, the 'mosaic' refers to:","opts":[["The mix of lipids, proteins and carbohydrates in the membrane",1,""],["Lipids moving sideways",0,"That's the 'fluid' part."],["The pattern of microvilli",0,"Not related."],["The two layers of the bilayer",0,"Not what mosaic means."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"Phospholipids with small heads and two hydrophobic tails, added to water, spontaneously form:","opts":[["A bilayer that closes into a liposome",1,"The liposome encloses an inner watery space, like a cell."],["Micelles",0,"Micelles form from lipids with a large head and one tail."],["A single layer on the bottom of the tube",0,"They arrange so the tails avoid water."],["Nothing; they need ATP",0,"Assembly is spontaneous."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"Which change makes a membrane more fluid?","opts":[["More C=C double bonds in the fatty acid tails",1,"Kinks stop tight packing."],["Longer fatty acid tails",0,"Longer tails pack more tightly: less fluid."],["Lower temperature",0,"Cooling reduces fluidity."],["Fewer double bonds",0,"Fewer double bonds: straighter tails, tighter packing."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"How does cholesterol affect membrane fluidity?","opts":[["It buffers it: it can increase or decrease fluidity depending on temperature",1,""],["It always makes the membrane stiffer",0,"At low temperature it keeps the membrane from packing solid."],["It always makes the membrane more fluid",0,"At high temperature it holds the membrane together."],["It has no effect",0,"It's a fluidity buffer."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"Lipid flip-flop (moving from one layer of the bilayer to the other) is:","opts":[["Rare, which lets the two layers differ in composition",1,""],["Constant, so both layers are identical",0,"It's rare."],["How lipids move sideways",0,"Sideways movement is lateral movement."],["Driven by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump",0,"Not related."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"33–36","q":"What are lipid rafts?","opts":[["Specialized microdomains of lipids and proteins that act as platforms for cell processes",1,""],["Vesicles that carry lipids to the Golgi",0,"Not what rafts are."],["Fat droplets in the cytoplasm",0,"Not what rafts are."],["Micelles inside the membrane",0,"Not what rafts are."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"45","q":"A red blood cell is placed in a hypertonic solution. What happens?","opts":[["Water leaves the cell and it shrinks",1,""],["Water enters and it bursts",0,"That's a hypotonic solution."],["Nothing changes",0,"That's isotonic."],["Salt enters and it swells",0,"Water moves, not the salt."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"45","q":"Osmosis is:","opts":[["Diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane",1,"Water moves toward the side with more solute."],["Active transport of water",0,"Water always moves passively."],["Diffusion of solute toward lower water concentration",0,"Osmosis is about water moving."],["Endocytosis of droplets",0,"That's pinocytosis."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"43–44","q":"How does a carrier protein differ from a channel protein?","opts":[["A carrier binds its cargo and changes shape; a channel forms a pore",1,"Carriers are slower and can be saturated."],["Carriers always use ATP",0,"Carriers can work passively (facilitated diffusion)."],["Channels only move glucose",0,"Channels mostly move ions and water."],["There is no difference",0,"They work differently."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"43–44","q":"Which is true of all passive transport?","opts":[["Substances move down their gradient with no energy spent by the cell",1,""],["It always needs a protein",0,"Simple diffusion doesn't."],["It can move substances against a gradient",0,"Only active transport does that."],["It only moves water",0,"It moves many substances."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"46–47","q":"The Na⁺/K⁺ pump moves:","opts":[["Na⁺ out of the cell and K⁺ into it, using ATP",1,"Both against their gradients: primary active transport."],["Na⁺ into the cell and K⁺ out of it",0,"Backwards."],["Na⁺ and K⁺ down their gradients",0,"It moves them up their gradients."],["Glucose into the cell",0,"That's the symporter."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"46–47","q":"The electrochemical gradient for an ion is made of:","opts":[["Its concentration gradient plus the charge difference across the membrane",1,""],["Only its concentration gradient",0,"Charge matters too."],["The pH of the cytosol",0,"Not the definition."],["The number of pumps",0,"Not the definition."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"Glucose enters an intestinal cell through the Na⁺/glucose symporter. What directly powers this?","opts":[["Na⁺ moving into the cell down its electrochemical gradient",1,"That's secondary active transport."],["ATP split by the symporter",0,"The symporter never uses ATP directly."],["Glucose moving down its gradient",0,"Glucose goes up its gradient here."],["The cell wall",0,"Animal cells don't have one."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"How does glucose leave the intestinal cell on the side facing the blood?","opts":[["Through GLUT, by facilitated diffusion",1,"Downhill, on the basolateral side."],["Through the Na⁺/glucose symporter",0,"That's on the apical (gut) side."],["By exocytosis",0,"Single glucose molecules aren't packaged in vesicles."],["Through the Na⁺/K⁺ pump",0,"The pump doesn't carry glucose."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"A Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger moves Na⁺ in and H⁺ out. It is:","opts":[["An antiporter (secondary active transport)",1,"Opposite directions, powered by the Na⁺ gradient."],["A symporter",0,"A symporter moves both the same way."],["A uniporter",0,"A uniporter moves one substance."],["A channel",0,"Channels don't couple two substances."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"Which process brings in specific molecules like LDL using receptors in coated pits?","opts":[["Receptor-mediated endocytosis",1,""],["Pinocytosis",0,"Pinocytosis is nonselective."],["Phagocytosis",0,"That engulfs large particles."],["Exocytosis",0,"That exports material."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"How does exocytosis relate to secretion?","opts":[["Vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside",1,"Secreted proteins leave the cell this way."],["It brings secreted molecules back in",0,"That's endocytosis."],["It's a type of passive transport",0,"Bulk transport needs energy."],["It only happens in plant cells",0,"All eukaryotic cells use it."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"Phagocytosis ends with the engulfed particle:","opts":[["In a phagosome that fuses with a lysosome to be digested",1,""],["Being released into the cytosol",0,"It stays inside a membrane."],["Exported again by exocytosis",0,"It's digested."],["Entering the nucleus",0,"Not where it goes."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"45","q":"A plant cell is placed in a hypertonic solution. What do your notes call what happens?","opts":[["Plasmolysis: the cell loses water and its membrane pulls away from the cell wall",1,"Water leaves by osmosis; the wall keeps its shape while the membrane shrinks away from it."],["Crenation: the cell shrivels into a spiky shape",0,"Crenation is the animal-cell (red blood cell) version."],["Lysis: the cell bursts",0,"Bursting happens in a hypotonic solution, and the wall prevents it in plants."],["Turgor: the cell presses against its wall",0,"That happens in a hypotonic solution."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"50","q":"After receptor-mediated endocytosis, where is the material delivered first?","opts":[["Early endosomes near the edge of the cell",1,"Then on to late endosomes deeper inside, which also receive material from the Golgi."],["Straight into the nucleus",0,"Endocytosed material stays in the endomembrane system."],["The rough ER",0,"Material moves from the ER outward, not the other way here."],["The cytosol, freely",0,"It stays inside vesicles and endosomes."]]}],"m4":[{"ch":0,"p":"53","q":"Griffith studied two strains of which bacterium?","opts":[["Streptococcus pneumoniae",1,"A virulent strain caused pneumonia and killed mice; a mutant nonvirulent strain did not."],["Escherichia coli",0,"Not the organism in Griffith's experiment."],["Bacillus subtilis",0,"Not the organism in Griffith's experiment."],["Staphylococcus aureus",0,"Not the organism in Griffith's experiment."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"54","q":"A mouse is injected with heat-killed virulent bacteria mixed with live nonvirulent bacteria. What happens, and why?","opts":[["It dies, because something in the dead virulent cells transformed the live nonvirulent cells",1,"That was Griffith's key result: transformation by an unknown molecule."],["It lives, because both types of bacteria are harmless on their own",0,"Each is harmless alone, but together the mouse dies."],["It dies, because heat-killed bacteria come back to life",0,"Heat-killed virulent bacteria alone do not kill the mouse."],["It lives, because the immune system destroys the dead cells first",0,"The mouse dies of pneumonia."]]},{"ch":0,"p":"54","q":"In the experiment by Avery, MacLeod and McCarty, which enzyme stopped the extract from transforming nonvirulent bacteria?","opts":[["DNase",1,"Destroying DNA was the only treatment that stopped transformation, so DNA carries the genetic information."],["RNase",0,"The RNase-treated extract still transformed the bacteria."],["Protease",0,"The protease-treated extract still transformed the bacteria."],["None of them; all tubes transformed",0,"The DNase tube did not transform."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Which carbon of deoxyribose is the base attached to?","opts":[["1′",1,"The base is on 1′, the phosphate on 5′, and the free –OH on 3′."],["2′",0,"In DNA the 2′ carbon has just –H."],["3′",0,"The 3′ carbon carries the –OH where the next nucleotide joins."],["5′",0,"The 5′ carbon carries the phosphate."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"What is the difference between a nucleoside and a nucleotide?","opts":[["A nucleoside is base + sugar; a nucleotide also has one or more phosphates",1,"NucleoTide has Three parts; nucleoSide has just base and Sugar."],["A nucleoside has a phosphate; a nucleotide does not",0,"It's the other way round."],["A nucleoside is found in RNA; a nucleotide in DNA",0,"Both terms apply to both."],["They are two names for the same molecule",0,"They differ by the phosphate."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Which bases are purines?","opts":[["Adenine and guanine",1,"Purines have two rings."],["Cytosine and thymine",0,"Those are pyrimidines (one ring)."],["Adenine and thymine",0,"Thymine is a pyrimidine."],["Guanine and uracil",0,"Uracil is a pyrimidine."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Where can a new nucleotide be added to a growing DNA strand?","opts":[["Only to the free 3′–OH, so strands grow 5′→3′",1,"Bases can only be added to the 3′–OH on the sugar."],["Only to the 5′ phosphate end",0,"The 5′ end has a phosphate, not a free –OH to attach to."],["To either end",0,"Nucleic acids only grow at the 3′ end."],["To the 2′ carbon",0,"The 2′ carbon is not part of the chain."]]},{"ch":1,"p":"55","q":"Why is the DNA backbone negatively charged?","opts":[["The phosphate groups' hydroxyls are ionized at cellular pH (about 7)",1,"Each phosphate carries negative charge."],["The bases are negatively charged",0,"The charge comes from the phosphates."],["Deoxyribose lacks an oxygen",0,"That does not create a charge."],["Hydrogen bonds carry charge",0,"Hydrogen bonds are weak attractions, not charges on the backbone."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"56","q":"What did Erwin Chargaff contribute to the DNA model?","opts":[["Base composition data showing %A = %T and %C = %G",1,"That pointed to A–T and C–G pairing."],["X-ray crystallography images of DNA",0,"That was Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins."],["The 3-D model of the double helix",0,"That was Watson and Crick."],["Proof that DNA is the genetic material",0,"That was Avery, MacLeod and McCarty."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"56","q":"A DNA sample is 20% thymine. What percent is cytosine?","opts":[["30%",1,"%T = %A = 20%, so A + T = 40%. The remaining 60% is split between C and G: 30% each."],["20%",0,"That is %A, not %C."],["40%",0,"40% is A + T together."],["60%",0,"60% is C + G together."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"57","q":"How many hydrogen bonds hold a G–C pair together?","opts":[["3",1,"G–C has three; A–T has two."],["2",0,"Two is A–T."],["1",0,"Every pair has more than one."],["4",0,"No base pair in DNA has four."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"57","q":"Which two factors keep the double helix stable?","opts":[["Hydrogen bonds between paired bases, and base stacking along each strand",1,"Many weak hydrogen bonds plus stacking interactions."],["Covalent bonds between the two strands, and histones",0,"The strands are not covalently joined to each other."],["Phosphodiester bonds between paired bases",0,"Phosphodiester bonds link nucleotides within a strand."],["Ionic bonds between phosphates",0,"The phosphates repel each other."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"56","q":"What does it mean that the two strands of DNA are antiparallel?","opts":[["They run in opposite directions: one 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′",1,""],["They never touch each other",0,"They are held together by base pairs."],["They have opposite charges",0,"Both backbones are negative."],["One is DNA and the other is RNA",0,"Both strands of a double helix are DNA."]]},{"ch":2,"p":"53","q":"What is a nucleosome?","opts":[["DNA wrapped around a core of histone proteins (H2A, H2B, H3, H4)",1,"Nucleosomes make up the 10 nm fibre."],["The region of the nucleus where ribosomes are made",0,"That is the nucleolus."],["A single nucleotide inside the nucleus",0,"A nucleosome is DNA plus histones."],["The fully condensed chromatid",0,"That is the last level of condensation (1400 nm)."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"58","q":"Which enzyme makes DNA using an RNA template?","opts":[["Reverse transcriptase",1,"An RNA-dependent DNA polymerase, used by HIV."],["RNA polymerase II",0,"That makes RNA from DNA."],["DNase",0,"DNase breaks DNA down."],["Ribosomes",0,"Ribosomes make protein."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"58","q":"What is gene expression?","opts":[["The production of a functional gene product, such as a protein",1,"Genes can be turned on (expressed) or off."],["Copying DNA before cell division",0,"That is replication."],["The full set of genetic material in an organism",0,"That is the genome."],["Removing introns from mRNA",0,"That is splicing."]]},{"ch":3,"p":"59","q":"Where do transcription and translation happen in a eukaryotic cell?","opts":[["Transcription in the nucleus, translation in the cytoplasm",1,"The nuclear envelope separates them."],["Both in the cytoplasm",0,"That is the prokaryotic arrangement."],["Both in the nucleus",0,"Ribosomes translate in the cytoplasm."],["Transcription in the cytoplasm, translation in the nucleus",0,"Backwards."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"60","q":"Which feature is typical of RNA but not DNA?","opts":[["A triphosphate at its 5′ end",1,"DNA's 5′ end is typically a monophosphate."],["Thymine",0,"Thymine is in DNA; RNA has uracil."],["Deoxyribose",0,"RNA has ribose."],["Two strands wound into a helix",0,"Most RNA is single-stranded."]]},{"ch":4,"p":"60","q":"Why is RNA less stable than DNA?","opts":[["Ribose has a hydroxyl (–OH) on its 2′ carbon",1,"The notes link the 2′ –OH to RNA's lower stability."],["Uracil has a methyl group",0,"Uracil has –H where thymine has –CH₃."],["RNA has no phosphates",0,"RNA has a sugar-phosphate backbone too."],["RNA is longer than DNA",0,"RNA is usually shorter."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"62","q":"The TATA box is:","opts":[["A common eukaryotic promoter sequence, 5′-TATAAA-3′",1,"The first transcribed nucleotide is usually about 25 bp away."],["The sequence where transcription ends",0,"That's a terminator."],["A sequence in mRNA recognized by ribosomes",0,"It is in DNA, in the promoter."],["The sequence enhancers bind to",0,"Activators bind enhancers; general transcription factors bind the promoter."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"62","q":"In bacteria, what directs RNA polymerase to the promoter?","opts":[["Sigma factor",1,"It associates with RNA polymerase and directs it to specific promoters."],["General transcription factors",0,"Those are eukaryotic."],["The mediator complex",0,"Mediator is eukaryotic."],["The 5′ cap",0,"The cap is added to eukaryotic mRNA."]]},{"ch":5,"p":"63","q":"How do activator proteins bound to a distant enhancer help start transcription?","opts":[["The DNA loops so the activators contact the mediator complex and the proteins at the promoter",1,""],["They travel along the DNA until they reach the promoter",0,"The notes describe looping."],["They become part of the RNA transcript",0,"They are proteins bound to DNA."],["They cut the DNA so Pol II can enter",0,"Nothing is cut."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"61","q":"In which direction does RNA polymerase read the template strand?","opts":[["3′→5′, so the RNA grows 5′→3′",1,""],["5′→3′, so the RNA grows 5′→3′",0,"The template and RNA are antiparallel."],["5′→3′, so the RNA grows 3′→5′",0,"RNA never grows 3′→5′."],["It depends on the gene",0,"Which strand is the template can change, but it's always read 3′→5′."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"64","q":"What is released each time a ribonucleotide is added to the growing RNA?","opts":[["Pyrophosphate (two phosphates)",1,"The 3′–OH attacks the innermost phosphate, and the outer two leave."],["ATP",0,"The incoming nucleotide supplies the energy itself."],["A water molecule only",0,"Pyrophosphate is released."],["The 5′ cap",0,"The cap is added in processing."]]},{"ch":6,"p":"63","q":"About how long is the transcription bubble, and how much of it is RNA–DNA duplex?","opts":[["About 14 bp, with about 8 bp of RNA–DNA duplex",1,""],["About 8 bp, with about 14 bp of duplex",0,"The duplex sits inside the bubble."],["The whole gene unwinds at once",0,"Only a short stretch is open."],["About 25 bp, with no duplex",0,"25 bp is the TATA box to start-site distance."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"65","q":"Polycistronic mRNA is:","opts":[["A prokaryotic mRNA carrying information for more than one gene",1,""],["A eukaryotic mRNA with many introns",0,"Introns are removed before translation."],["An mRNA with several 5′ caps",0,"No mRNA has several caps."],["An mRNA made by reverse transcriptase",0,"Reverse transcriptase makes DNA."]]},{"ch":7,"p":"65","q":"Why can translation start before transcription is finished in bacteria?","opts":[["There's no nuclear envelope, and the primary transcript is already the mRNA",1,"Transcription and translation are coupled."],["Bacterial ribosomes are inside the nucleus",0,"Bacteria have no nucleus."],["Bacteria splice mRNA faster",0,"Bacterial primary transcripts aren't processed."],["Bacterial RNA polymerase also makes protein",0,"Ribosomes make protein."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What is the 5′ cap?","opts":[["A 7-methylguanosine joined to the 5′ end by a 5′–5′ triphosphate bridge",1,"It protects the mRNA and lets the ribosome recognize it."],["About 250 adenines added to the 5′ end",0,"That's the poly(A) tail, on the 3′ end."],["The first exon of the gene",0,"The cap is added after transcription."],["A protein that binds the TATA box",0,"That's a transcription factor."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"66","q":"Which is a role of the poly(A) tail?","opts":[["Export of the mRNA to the cytoplasm",1,"It also helps transcription termination and protects against exonucleases."],["Letting the ribosome recognize the 5′ end",0,"That's the cap."],["Binding sigma factor",0,"Sigma factor is bacterial and binds promoters."],["Removing introns",0,"Splicing removes introns."]]},{"ch":8,"p":"66","q":"A gene's primary transcript has exons 1 to 4. One cell makes mRNA with exons 1, 2 and 4; another with exons 1, 2 and 3. What is this?","opts":[["Alternative splicing",1,"One gene, different related proteins in different cells."],["Polyadenylation",0,"That adds the poly(A) tail."],["Reverse transcription",0,"That makes DNA from RNA."],["Polycistronic mRNA",0,"That's a prokaryotic mRNA with several genes."]]}]},"review":[{"mod":"m1","ch":0,"p":"4","q":"Who was the first person to observe and name \"cells,\" and what material was he examining?","a":"Robert Hooke, in 1665. He looked at thin slices of cork and described its chambers as \"cells\". He was actually seeing empty walls, the remains of dead cells."},{"mod":"m1","ch":0,"p":"4","q":"What was Antonie van Leeuwenhoek's contribution to cell biology, and how did his observations differ from those of Robert Hooke?","a":"Leeuwenhoek (1673–1700) was an early light microscopist who improved the magnifying power of microscope lenses. He saw living \"animalcules\" in pond water. Hooke saw the empty walls of dead cork cells; Leeuwenhoek saw living, moving cells."},{"mod":"m1","ch":1,"p":"5","q":"What are the three main tenets of the cell theory? Who proposed the first two tenets? Who proposed the third tenet?","a":"1. All living organisms are composed of one (unicellular) or more (multicellular) cells. 2. Each cell is the structural and functional unit of life. 3. Cells can arise only from pre-existing cells. Schleiden and Schwann proposed the first two; Virchow proposed the third."},{"mod":"m1","ch":1,"p":"4–5","q":"Who contributed to the development of the cell theory?","a":"Hooke (named cells in cork, 1665) and Leeuwenhoek (saw living cells) made the first observations. Schleiden, a botanist, noted that all plant tissues are made of cells and plant embryos arise from a single cell. Schwann, a zoologist, reached the same conclusion for animals and saw that plants and animals are similar. Virchow, a physician, added the third tenet."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What features are shared by all cells?","a":"All cells are highly complex and organized and enclosed by a plasma membrane; have a genetic program (DNA, read through the central dogma); can produce more of themselves (duplicating their DNA before division); acquire and use energy; carry out chemical reactions (metabolism); engage in mechanical activities; respond to stimuli (receptors); and regulate themselves."},{"mod":"m1","ch":2,"p":"5","q":"What are exceptions to the cell theory?","a":"Viruses, viroids and prions. None is made of cells or can reproduce on its own."},{"mod":"m1","ch":2,"p":"5","q":"How do viruses differ from living cells in terms of structure, replication, and dependence on host cells?","a":"Structure: a virus is just a small amount of nucleic acid (DNA or RNA) in a protein coat (capsid), sometimes with an envelope; it has no membrane-bound cytoplasm, ribosomes or metabolism. Replication: it cannot divide; it injects its genome into a host cell, which copies it and builds new viruses. Dependence: outside a living cell a virus is inert; it needs a host cell's machinery to reproduce."},{"mod":"m1","ch":2,"p":"5","q":"What are viroids? What are prions?","a":"Viroids are small circular RNAs with no protein coat that need host machinery to replicate (e.g. potato spindle tuber viroid). Prions are proteinaceous infectious particles with no nucleic acid: a misfolded prion protein (PrP^Sc) converts normal protein (PrP^C) into the misfolded form, which accumulates."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"What is the difference between the cytoplasm and the cytosol, and what roles do they play in cellular function?","a":"The cytoplasm is all the contents inside the cell membrane. The cytosol is the jelly-like internal fluid: the cytoplasm minus the organelles, containing water, dissolved ions and molecules. The cytosol is where many of the cell's chemical reactions happen and where organelles sit and materials move."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"How do cells maintain a boundary between their internal environment and the outside world?","a":"Every cell is enclosed by a physical barrier, the cell (plasma) membrane. Inside it is the cytoplasm. Bacteria, plants and fungi also have a cell wall outside the membrane for support."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"How do cells store, express, and transmit genetic information?","a":"Information is stored in DNA. A gene is the DNA code for a functional product. It is expressed through the central dogma: DNA is transcribed into RNA, and RNA is translated into protein. Cells transmit it by duplicating their genetic material before they divide."},{"mod":"m1","ch":4,"p":"6–9","q":"How do prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells differ in structure and complexity?","a":"Prokaryotes (Bacteria and Archaea) are single cells with no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles: a single circular chromosome in a nucleoid, often plasmids, ribosomes, a membrane and (bacteria) a cell wall. They are small and divide by binary fission. Eukaryotes have a membrane-bound nucleus holding their chromosomes and many specialized organelles (endomembrane system, mitochondria, chloroplasts in plants), and are usually much larger and more complex."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"6","q":"How is genetic information stored, transmitted, and expressed in cells?","a":"Stored as DNA; transmitted by copying the DNA before cell division so each new cell gets a full set; expressed by transcription of genes into RNA and translation of RNA into protein (the central dogma)."},{"mod":"m1","ch":3,"p":"3, 6–9","q":"What is the relationship between structure and function in biological systems?","a":"A structure's shape and makeup fit the job it does. Examples from Module 1: the plasma membrane forms a barrier around every cell; the cell wall gives bacteria and plants rigid support; the plant vacuole holds water to make turgor pressure; chloroplasts capture sunlight; mitochondria make ATP; the cytoskeleton gives an internal framework."},{"mod":"m1","ch":5,"p":"8","q":"What are protists? What are fungi?","a":"Protists are a diverse group of eukaryotes, mostly single cells though some form colonies: algae (Volvox), water molds (potato blight), slime molds and protozoa (ciliates). Fungi are eukaryotes with cell walls containing chitin (a modified polysaccharide): yeasts, rusts, smuts, mildews, molds and mushrooms. They are heterotrophs that absorb preformed organic molecules directly from the environment."},{"mod":"m1","ch":5,"p":"9","q":"How do plant cells differ structurally from animal cells, and what is the function of these differences (e.g., cell wall, chloroplasts, large central vacuole)?","a":"Plant cells have a cell wall of polysaccharides (a rigid barrier for support), a large water-filled vacuole (structural rigidity through turgor pressure), chloroplasts (capture sunlight to make sugars: plants are autotrophs) and plasmodesmata (connect neighbouring cells). Animal cells have no cell wall, are heterotrophs and have a distinct mode of early development."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What is the cytoskeleton, and what are its main functions in eukaryotic cells?","a":"A network of protein filaments and associated proteins that gives the cell an internal structural framework. It supports the cell's shape and helps with mechanical activities such as moving materials within the cell. It is not part of the endomembrane system."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What is the nuclear envelope, and how does it separate the nucleus from the cytoplasm?","a":"The nuclear envelope is the membrane around the nucleus, part of the endomembrane system. It encloses the chromosomes, separating the genetic material and RNA synthesis from the cytoplasm. Pores in it let molecules such as RNA move in and out (covered in more detail in Module 3)."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"How do ribosomes, the endoplasmic reticulum, and the Golgi apparatus work together to synthesize and process proteins?","a":"Ribosomes make proteins. Ribosomes on the rough ER make proteins that enter the ER, which is involved in protein (and lipid) synthesis. Vesicles carry them to the Golgi apparatus, which modifies and sorts them and sends them on in vesicles to their final destination, inside or outside the cell."},{"mod":"m1","ch":6,"p":"9","q":"What is the primary function of mitochondria? How about lysosomes? How about peroxisomes?","a":"Mitochondria produce most of the ATP, the cell's energy currency. Lysosomes contain enzymes that break down macromolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, carbohydrates). Peroxisomes break down specific organic molecules such as fatty acids and synthesize others, such as cholesterol and some phospholipids."},{"mod":"m1","ch":7,"p":"10","q":"How do scientists investigate biological questions using model organisms? What are some examples of model organisms?","a":"Model organisms are non-human species studied to understand biology in general, because all cells share basic properties. Examples: E. coli (DNA replication, transcription, translation), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (cell cycle; minimal model eukaryote), Arabidopsis thaliana (flowering plants), Drosophila melanogaster (genetics, development), Caenorhabditis elegans (first animal genome sequenced; fate of every cell known), Mus musculus (model mammal)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":0,"p":"13","q":"What are atoms, and what particles are they made of?","a":"An atom is the basic unit of matter; each element contains only one type of atom. Every atom has a dense central nucleus of positively charged protons and neutral neutrons, with negatively charged electrons moving around the nucleus in orbitals (at most 2 electrons per orbital)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":0,"p":"13","q":"What are valence electrons, and why are they important for the formation of chemical bonds in biological molecules?","a":"Valence electrons are the electrons in an atom's outermost orbitals. Their number determines how many bonds the atom can make (carbon 4, nitrogen 3, oxygen 2, hydrogen 1). Atoms share or transfer valence electrons to combine into molecules."},{"mod":"m2","ch":1,"p":"14–16","q":"How do covalent, ionic, and hydrogen bonds differ in strength and behavior?","a":"Covalent bonds share valence electrons in a molecular orbital; they are the strong bonds of biological systems. Ionic bonds form when one atom takes an electron from another, leaving oppositely charged ions held together by attraction (they come apart in water, as when NaCl dissolves). Hydrogen bonds are weak attractions between an electronegative atom and a hydrogen already covalently bonded to another atom; they constantly break and re-form, as between water molecules."},{"mod":"m2","ch":1,"p":"14","q":"What is electronegativity, and how does it influence bond polarity?","a":"Electronegativity is how strongly an atom attracts shared electrons. If two atoms have the same or nearly the same electronegativity, they share equally: a nonpolar covalent bond. If one is more electronegative (like O in water), it pulls the electrons closer, gaining a slight negative charge and leaving the other slightly positive: a polar covalent bond. A very large difference leads to an ionic bond."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"16","q":"How do polar and nonpolar molecules interact differently with water?","a":"Polar and charged molecules are attracted to water's partial charges and dissolve: they are hydrophilic. Nonpolar molecules can't form these attractions, so water molecules hydrogen-bond to each other and push the nonpolar molecules together: they are hydrophobic. Amphipathic molecules have both kinds of region and form structures like micelles and bilayers."},{"mod":"m2","ch":1,"p":"15","q":"What is the difference between a cation and an anion? What happens to sodium chloride (NaCl) when it dissolves in water, and how do the properties of water molecules facilitate this process?","a":"A cation is positively charged (an extra proton relative to its electrons, e.g. Na⁺); an anion is negatively charged (an extra electron, e.g. Cl⁻). When NaCl is added to water, Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are pulled apart and each becomes surrounded by water molecules. Water is polar: the partial negative oxygens face Na⁺ and the partial positive hydrogens face Cl⁻, so NaCl dissolves."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"14, 16","q":"What is the structure of a water molecule, and how does it lead to hydrogen bonding?","a":"Water is one oxygen covalently bonded to two hydrogens in an angular (bent) shape. Oxygen is more electronegative, so it carries a slight negative charge and each hydrogen a slight positive charge. The δ⁺ hydrogen of one molecule is attracted to the δ⁻ oxygen of a neighbour: a hydrogen bond."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"16","q":"How do the properties of water support life?","a":"Water is the medium of life and the most abundant molecule in organisms. Because it is polar, it dissolves more kinds of molecules than any other liquid, letting reactions happen in solution. Its interactions with hydrophilic, hydrophobic and amphipathic molecules shape structures such as membranes. Its slight dissociation into H⁺ and OH⁻ sets pH, which affects proteins and enzymes."},{"mod":"m2","ch":2,"p":"16","q":"What is the difference between hydrophilic and hydrophobic molecules?","a":"Hydrophilic (\"water loving\") molecules are highly attracted to water; they are polar or charged, like salts and sugars. Hydrophobic (\"water fearing\") molecules are not attracted to water; they are nonpolar, like oils and hydrocarbon chains."},{"mod":"m2","ch":3,"p":"17","q":"What is pH? What type of solution has a pH lower than 7? How about higher than 7?","a":"pH measures the concentration of protons (H⁺) in a solution, on a scale from 0 to 14; pure water is 7. A pH lower than 7 is acidic (more H⁺). Higher than 7 is basic (less H⁺). Each step of 1 is a tenfold change. pH matters for cell function, such as protein structure and enzyme activity."},{"mod":"m2","ch":4,"p":"18","q":"Why is carbon uniquely suited to form the backbone of biological molecules?","a":"Carbon can form four covalent bonds, in many combinations of single and double bonds, with many other elements. It can link into chains or rings, giving a huge variety of molecules. In methane its four bonds point to the corners of a tetrahedron."},{"mod":"m2","ch":5,"p":"19","q":"How are macromolecules assembled from monomers?","a":"Macromolecules are large molecules built from smaller organic molecules. Many are polymers of repeating monomers: amino acids make proteins, nucleotides make nucleic acids, monosaccharides make polysaccharides. Monomers are joined by covalent bonds in condensation reactions, each releasing one water. Specific enzymes speed this up."},{"mod":"m2","ch":5,"p":"19","q":"What is condensation reaction, and how does it differ from hydrolysis?","a":"Condensation (making the chain) joins two monomers with a covalent bond and releases a water molecule; it requires energy. Hydrolysis (breaking the chain) inserts a water molecule across the bond to split it; it releases energy. Both are sped up by enzymes."},{"mod":"m2","ch":4,"p":"20","q":"What are functional groups, and what are some common types found in biological molecules?","a":"Functional groups are groups of atoms attached to carbon chains that give them their own chemical properties. Groups with N, O, P or S are polar: amino (–NH₂, basic, +), amide, carboxyl (–COOH, acidic, –), carbonyl (C=O), hydroxyl (–OH), sulfhydryl (–SH, forms disulfide bonds) and phosphate (–). The methyl group (–CH₃) is nonpolar. They take part in the reactions that build polymers."},{"mod":"m2","ch":8,"p":"26–28","q":"What are the monomers and functions of carbohydrates? How do simple sugars differ from complex carbohydrates?","a":"The monomers are monosaccharides (simple sugars, 5 or 6 carbons, C:H:O about 1:2:1, mostly in ring form; e.g. glucose, galactose, fructose). Carbohydrates are a major energy source and also structural (cellulose). Simple sugars are single units; complex carbohydrates are many units linked by glycosidic bonds: disaccharides (2, e.g. sucrose), oligosaccharides (a few, often on proteins or lipids) and polysaccharides (many: starch and glycogen for storage, cellulose for structure)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":9,"p":"29–30","q":"What are lipids, and how do their structures affect their functions? How do phospholipids contribute to membrane formation? What is the difference between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids?","a":"Lipids are a diverse group grouped by being hydrophobic; they are not polymers. Triacylglycerols (glycerol + three fatty acids) store energy. Steroids (four fused rings, e.g. cholesterol) sit in animal membranes and make hormones. Phospholipids have a polar phosphate head and two nonpolar tails; being amphipathic, they arrange with heads to water and tails inside, forming membranes. Saturated fatty acids have no C=C double bonds (straight); unsaturated ones have one or more (kinked)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":6,"p":"21–22","q":"How do proteins differ in structure and function? What roles do proteins play in catalysis, structure, transport, and signaling?","a":"Proteins are polymers of amino acids (20 kinds, differing in their R groups) joined by peptide bonds; the order of amino acids determines the protein. They have more functions than any other macromolecule: enzymes catalyze reactions; transport proteins move substances across membranes and in circulation; signalling proteins include hormones, receptors and messengers; others give movement (motor proteins, flagella), support (cytoskeleton, extracellular matrix) and defense (antibodies)."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23–24","q":"What are nucleotides, and what are their components? What is the difference between DNA and RNA?","a":"Nucleotides are the monomers of nucleic acids, with three parts: a nitrogenous base, a five-carbon sugar and one or more phosphate groups. DNA uses deoxyribose (–H on the 2′ carbon) and bases A, G, C, T; it is the genetic material. RNA uses ribose (–OH on the 2′ carbon) and bases A, G, C, U; it works in protein synthesis and gene regulation."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23","q":"How do nitrogenous bases pair in DNA and RNA?","a":"Module 2 introduces the bases; pairing is covered in Module 4. A purine always pairs with a pyrimidine: in DNA, A pairs with T and G pairs with C. In RNA, U takes the place of T, so A pairs with U."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"25","q":"How is a DNA strand oriented, and what does 5′ to 3′ mean?","a":"Phosphodiester bonds link the phosphate on the 5′ carbon of one nucleotide to the 3′–OH of the next, giving the strand direction. The chain begins at the 5′ end (with a free phosphate) and new nucleotides are added to the free 3′–OH end, so chains are always built 5′ → 3′."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23","q":"What role do nucleic acids play in storing and transmitting genetic information?","a":"Nucleic acids are information molecules: the order of their nitrogenous bases encodes genetic information. DNA is the genetic material of all cellular organisms and directs protein synthesis; RNA carries out protein synthesis and regulates gene expression."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"23","q":"What are the structural differences between purines and pyrimidines?","a":"Purines (adenine and guanine) have a double ring. Pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine and uracil) have a single ring."},{"mod":"m2","ch":7,"p":"24","q":"What is ATP, and why is it important in cellular processes?","a":"ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a nucleotide: adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups. Cleaving off the terminal phosphate releases stored energy (ATP → ADP + Pᵢ + energy), so ATP transfers energy in metabolism and powers cell work."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"33","q":"How does the structure of cellular membranes allow them to fuse together without losing their overall continuity?","a":"Membranes are continuous, unbroken sheets of phospholipid bilayer that enclose compartments. Because the bilayer is held together by hydrophobic interactions rather than fixed bonds, and the lipids can move, two bilayers can merge into one and phospholipids spontaneously rearrange to close any gap. That is why a vesicle can fuse with the plasma membrane (or bud from the ER) without leaving a hole, and why membranes are self-healing."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"33","q":"What are the various functions of membranes?","a":"1. Compartmentalization (compartments have different contents and activities). 2. A selectively permeable barrier that prevents unrestricted exchange of molecules. 3. Transporting solutes across the membrane. 4. A scaffold for biochemical activities. 5. Responding to external signals (signal transduction). 6. Intercellular interaction with neighbouring cells. 7. Energy transduction, converting one form of energy into another."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"34","q":"What is the structure of a phospholipid? How do phospholipids arrange themselves?","a":"A phospholipid has a glycerol backbone attached to a polar phosphate-containing head group and two nonpolar fatty acid tails. In water, the hydrophilic heads face the water and the hydrophobic tails hide from it. Depending on their shape they form micelles (single layer sphere) or bilayers. Phospholipids added to water spontaneously form closed bilayers called liposomes, and the cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with tails inside and heads on both surfaces."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"34","q":"How does the amphipathic nature of phospholipids contribute to membrane formation?","a":"Amphipathic means having both a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic region. The polar heads are attracted to water while the nonpolar tails are pushed together away from it. So phospholipids arrange themselves spontaneously into a bilayer with the tails in the middle and the heads facing the watery cytosol and extracellular fluid. No energy is needed, and the bilayer reseals itself if disturbed."},{"mod":"m3","ch":3,"p":"35","q":"How can membrane fluidity be affected? What are lipid rafts?","a":"Lipids move laterally within the membrane, and how freely depends on the tails. Longer fatty acid tails make the membrane less fluid. Fewer carbon–carbon double bonds (more saturated, straighter tails that pack tightly) make it less fluid; more double bonds (kinks) make it more fluid. Cholesterol in animal membranes can increase or decrease fluidity depending on temperature. Lipid rafts are discrete regions of the membrane where certain lipids and proteins gather together."},{"mod":"m3","ch":2,"p":"36","q":"What is the fluid mosaic model of membrane structure?","a":"The core of the membrane is a lipid bilayer that is fluid: its molecules can move laterally. Embedded in and attached to it is a mosaic (mixture) of different molecules: lipids, proteins and carbohydrates. Membranes are dynamic structures whose components are mobile and can come together in transient interactions."},{"mod":"m3","ch":2,"p":"36","q":"What types of proteins are found in cell membranes, and what are their functions? How about membrane carbohydrates?","a":"Integral membrane proteins are permanently associated with the membrane and can span the whole bilayer (transmembrane proteins). Peripheral membrane proteins are temporarily attached to the bilayer surface or to integral proteins by noncovalent bonds, on the cytoplasmic or extracellular side. Their functions: transporters that move ions and molecules, receptors that receive signals, enzymes that catalyze reactions, and anchors that attach to other proteins. Membrane carbohydrates all face outward and are covalently linked to proteins (glycoproteins) or lipids (glycolipids)."},{"mod":"m3","ch":5,"p":"43–46","q":"What are the differences between passive and active transport?","a":"Passive transport moves substances down their concentration gradient (high to low) by diffusion, either directly through the bilayer or through a protein, and needs no energy input. Active transport moves substances against their concentration gradient (low to high) using a carrier protein and a source of energy: ATP directly (primary) or an existing electrochemical gradient (secondary)."},{"mod":"m3","ch":5,"p":"43","q":"What is diffusion, and how does it move substances across membranes?","a":"Diffusion is the spontaneous net movement of molecules from an area of high concentration to an area of low concentration. Net movement stops when there is no concentration gradient, though random motion continues both ways. Small nonpolar molecules (O₂, CO₂, lipids) and small uncharged polar molecules like water can diffuse directly across the bilayer: simple diffusion."},{"mod":"m3","ch":5,"p":"44","q":"What is facilitated diffusion, and what role do channel and carrier proteins play?","a":"Facilitated diffusion is movement down the concentration gradient through a protein transporter. Channel proteins are integral proteins that surround an aqueous pore, are selective for specific molecules, and can be gated open or closed. Carrier proteins bind a specific molecule on one side, change conformation, and release it on the other side, where it diffuses away down its gradient."},{"mod":"m3","ch":4,"p":"45","q":"How does osmosis differ from simple diffusion? What happens to cells in hypotonic, isotonic, and hypertonic solutions?","a":"Osmosis is the diffusion of water across a semi-permeable membrane, from higher water concentration (lower solute) to lower water concentration (higher solute). Isotonic: equal on both sides, no net water movement. Hypertonic (more solute outside): water leaves, the cell shrinks; animal cells shrivel (crenation), plant cells pull away from the wall (plasmolysis). Hypotonic (less solute outside): water enters, the cell swells; animal cells can burst (lysis; hemolysis in red blood cells), plant cells push against the wall (turgor)."},{"mod":"m3","ch":6,"p":"46","q":"What is active transport, and how does it use energy? How does the sodium-potassium pump work?","a":"Active transport moves a substance against its gradient using a carrier that changes conformation, so movement is one-way. The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (in animal cells) is primary active transport. Na⁺ ions bind on the inside; ATP is hydrolyzed and the pump is phosphorylated; the pump changes shape and releases Na⁺ outside; K⁺ binds on the outside; the phosphate is removed; the pump snaps back and releases K⁺ inside. Your notes give the ratio as 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per cycle, both against their gradients."},{"mod":"m3","ch":7,"p":"47–48","q":"What is secondary active transport, and how is it different from primary active transport?","a":"Primary active transport uses the chemical energy of ATP directly (e.g. the Na⁺/K⁺ pump or a proton pump). Secondary active transport (co-transport) uses an electrochemical gradient that a primary pump built: an ion moving down its gradient drives another molecule against its gradient. Symporters move both in the same direction (Na⁺ and glucose); antiporters move them in opposite directions (Na⁺ and H⁺). In intestinal cells, Na⁺ entering at the apical membrane drives glucose in; glucose then leaves at the basal membrane by facilitated diffusion."},{"mod":"m3","ch":6,"p":"47","q":"What is an electrochemical gradient?","a":"A gradient across a membrane with both a chemical part (a difference in concentration) and an electrical part (a difference in charge). Pumping ions such as Na⁺ or H⁺ builds one; the inside of the cell is negative relative to the outside. Its stored energy can drive secondary active transport."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"37–38","q":"What is the endomembrane system? Which are found in a typical animal cell? How about a plant cell?","a":"The endomembrane system is the set of interconnected membranes and organelles, linked physically or by vesicles: nuclear envelope, ER, Golgi apparatus, lysosomes, vesicles and the plasma membrane. A typical animal cell has ER, Golgi, lysosomes, mitochondria and a cytoskeleton. A plant cell has the same plus a cell wall for support, vacuoles that maintain turgor pressure, and chloroplasts that capture light energy."},{"mod":"m3","ch":8,"p":"49–50","q":"What is endocytosis, and how does it allow cells to internalize substances? What is the difference between phagocytosis, pinocytosis, and receptor-mediated endocytosis? What are the steps for each process?","a":"In endocytosis the membrane folds inward around material, the pocket pinches off, and the material ends up inside a new vesicle. Phagocytosis: a large particle binds the surface, the membrane surrounds it to form a phagosome, which fuses with a lysosome; enzymes digest the contents (nutrition or defense, e.g. macrophages and neutrophils). Pinocytosis (cell drinking): extracellular fluid is taken in with no recognition, forming small vesicles that don't need a lysosome. Receptor-mediated endocytosis: specific molecules bind receptors concentrated in a coated pit, clathrin on the cytoplasmic side pulls it in as a coated vesicle, and the material goes to early then late endosomes."},{"mod":"m3","ch":8,"p":"49","q":"What is exocytosis, and how does it relate to secretion?","a":"Exocytosis is when a vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases its contents outside the cell. Vesicles that bud off the endomembrane system (from the Golgi) carry secreted proteins this way, so exocytosis is how cells secrete substances. It also adds the vesicle's membrane and proteins to the plasma membrane."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"38","q":"What is the structure and function of the nucleus? What is the nuclear envelope, and what is the role of nuclear pores?","a":"The nucleus holds the cell's DNA (chromosomes) and is where RNA is made; the nucleolus inside makes ribosome parts. The nuclear envelope is a double membrane (inner and outer) that separates the nucleus from the cytoplasm and is continuous with the ER. Nuclear pores are protein openings in the envelope that let molecules such as RNA and proteins move in and out."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"38–39","q":"What are the roles of the rough and smooth endoplasmic reticulum?","a":"The rough ER has ribosomes on its cytosolic face and makes proteins that go into membranes, are secreted, or go to the Golgi and lysosomes. The smooth ER lacks ribosomes; it makes lipids, and parts of it bud off as vesicles that carry material onward to the Golgi."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"39","q":"What is the Golgi apparatus, and what is its role in the cell?","a":"The Golgi is a stack of flattened membrane sacs (cisternae), not attached to the ER, that receives vesicles from the ER. It modifies proteins and lipids (for example adding sugars), then sorts and ships them in vesicles to the plasma membrane, to lysosomes, or out of the cell. It is the cell's sorting station."},{"mod":"m3","ch":0,"p":"40","q":"What are lysosomes and what is their function?","a":"Lysosomes are specialized vesicles derived from the Golgi. They contain enzymes (made in the RER, sorted in the Golgi) that degrade damaged or unneeded proteins, nucleic acids, lipids and complex carbohydrates. A proton pump in their membrane keeps the inside at about pH 5."},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"41","q":"What organelles are not part of the endomembrane system?","a":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts are membranous organelles that are not part of the endomembrane system. They are semi-autonomous: they grow and divide on their own and have their own circular genomes. (Peroxisomes, ribosomes and the cytoskeleton are also outside it.)"},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"41","q":"How does the structure of mitochondria relate to their function?","a":"Mitochondria convert the energy in sugars into ATP. They have an outer membrane and a highly folded inner membrane; the folds (cristae) give a large surface area for the reactions that make ATP. The inner membrane encloses the matrix, which holds enzymes, ribosomes and the mitochondrion's own DNA."},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"41–42","q":"What is the role of chloroplasts in plant cells? What is their structure?","a":"Chloroplasts capture light energy and convert it into chemical energy (sugars) by photosynthesis. They have a double membrane (outer and inner) and an internal membrane system of flattened sacs called thylakoids, which hold pigments such as chlorophyll. Thylakoids are stacked into grana; the space inside a thylakoid is the lumen, and the fluid around them is the stroma."},{"mod":"m3","ch":1,"p":"42","q":"How do mitochondria and chloroplasts support the endosymbiotic theory?","a":"Both resemble bacteria in organization and biochemistry: they have a double membrane, their own circular DNA and ribosomes, and they grow and divide on their own. Chloroplasts resemble cyanobacteria (photosynthetic bacteria) and mitochondria resemble proteobacteria. The theory says each began as a bacterium engulfed by a host cell that kept it as an endosymbiont."},{"mod":"m4","ch":0,"p":"53","q":"How is the structure of DNA linked to its function?","a":"DNA has two jobs: storing genetic information and copying itself to pass that information on. Its sequence of bases stores the information, and complementary base pairing (A–T, G–C) means each strand can act as a guide for building a new partner strand, so the information can be copied."},{"mod":"m4","ch":0,"p":"53–54","q":"How do we know DNA is the genetic material? Describe the experiments conducted by Griffith and Avery, MacLeod & McCarty.","a":"Griffith (1928) used two strains of Streptococcus pneumoniae. Live virulent bacteria killed mice; nonvirulent ones did not; heat-killed virulent bacteria did not; but heat-killed virulent mixed with live nonvirulent bacteria killed the mouse. Something in the debris transformed the nonvirulent cells, but the molecule wasn't identified. Avery, MacLeod and McCarty (1944) took an extract of DNA, RNA and protein from heat-killed virulent bacteria and treated it with DNase, RNase or protease. Only DNase stopped transformation, showing DNA is the transforming molecule."},{"mod":"m4","ch":1,"p":"55, 60","q":"What are the building blocks of DNA and RNA? What is a nucleotide vs. nucleoside? What are the bases in DNA? RNA?","a":"Both are polymers of nucleotides. A nucleotide has three parts: a five-carbon sugar (deoxyribose in DNA, ribose in RNA), a base attached to the 1′ carbon, and one or more phosphates. A nucleoside is just base plus sugar. DNA bases: A, T, C, G. RNA bases: A, U, C, G (uracil replaces thymine)."},{"mod":"m4","ch":1,"p":"55","q":"What is a phosphodiester bond? How does one end of a strand of DNA differ from the other? Where are bases added to a growing chain?","a":"A phosphodiester bond links the 3′ carbon of one sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next through a phosphate, forming the sugar-phosphate backbone. One end of the strand has a free 5′ phosphate, the other a free 3′ hydroxyl (polarity). New nucleotides can only be added to the 3′–OH, so strands grow 5′→3′."},{"mod":"m4","ch":4,"p":"60","q":"How do DNA and RNA differ in structure and function?","a":"DNA uses deoxyribose and thymine, has a 5′ monophosphate, is very large and double-stranded, and stores genetic information. RNA uses ribose (with a 2′ –OH, making it less stable) and uracil, has a 5′ triphosphate, is smaller and usually single-stranded, can fold into complex shapes, and carries out the information (mRNA carries a gene's code to the ribosome)."},{"mod":"m4","ch":2,"p":"56","q":"What is the significance of Watson & Crick? How about Franklin & Wilkins? How about Chargaff?","a":"Watson and Crick built the 3-D double helix model. They based it on Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins's X-ray crystallography of DNA and on Erwin Chargaff's finding that %A = %T and %C = %G."},{"mod":"m4","ch":2,"p":"56–57","q":"Why is there specificity of base pairing? What are complementary base pairs?","a":"Specificity comes from hydrogen bonding: A and T form two hydrogen bonds, C and G form three, and a purine always pairs with a pyrimidine. A–T and G–C are complementary pairs: knowing one strand tells you the sequence of the other."},{"mod":"m4","ch":2,"p":"57","q":"What is the role of hydrogen bonding in stabilizing the DNA double helix? How about base stacking?","a":"Hydrogen bonds are weak individually but together hold the two strands together, and because they are weak the DNA can unzip down the middle. Base stacking is the interaction between neighbouring bases on the same strand; it also stabilizes the helix."},{"mod":"m4","ch":3,"p":"58","q":"What is the central dogma of molecular biology? What is a genome? What is gene expression?","a":"Central dogma: DNA is transcribed into RNA, which is translated into protein (reverse transcription is an exception). A genome is the genetic material of an organism (or compartment, like the mitochondrial genome). Gene expression is the production of a functional gene product, such as a protein; genes can be turned on or off, and expression is regulated."},{"mod":"m4","ch":3,"p":"58–59","q":"What is transcription, and what is its overall purpose? Where does it occur in prokaryotes? In eukaryotes?","a":"Transcription makes an RNA copy of a DNA sequence, using DNA as the template, so the gene's information can be used (for example, mRNA directing protein synthesis). In prokaryotes it happens in the cytoplasm; in eukaryotes, in the nucleus."},{"mod":"m4","ch":4,"p":"60","q":"What is RNA? What are the similarity and differences between RNA & DNA?","a":"RNA is a polymer of ribonucleotides. Like DNA it has a sugar-phosphate backbone and polarity (5′ phosphate end, 3′ hydroxyl end). Differences: ribose vs deoxyribose, U vs T (A pairs with U), 5′ triphosphate vs monophosphate, shorter, mostly single-stranded, and can fold into complex shapes."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"61","q":"What is needed for transcription to occur? What is the template strand of DNA? What are ribonucleotide triphosphates and why are they needed for transcription?","a":"A DNA template, RNA polymerase, and ribonucleotide triphosphates. The template strand is the DNA strand that RNA polymerase reads; the RNA is complementary to it. Ribonucleotide triphosphates (ATP, GTP, CTP, UTP) are the building blocks, and their phosphates provide the energy for the synthesis reaction."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"62","q":"What enzyme is responsible for synthesizing RNA from a DNA template in prokaryotes? How about in eukaryotes?","a":"RNA polymerase. Prokaryotes use a single type of RNA polymerase for all transcription. Eukaryotes have three; RNA polymerase II makes mRNA."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"61–62","q":"How does transcription take place? What is a promoter, and how does it initiate transcription? How does RNA polymerase identify the template strand?","a":"A region of DNA unwinds and one strand is used as the template; RNA grows 5′→3′ as the template is read 3′→5′, in three stages (initiation, elongation, termination). The promoter is a conserved double-stranded DNA sequence where transcription factors and RNA polymerase bind, which sets where and in which direction transcription starts (in eukaryotes, often a TATA box about 25 bp upstream of the start). The promoter's orientation decides which strand is the template, so it can differ from gene to gene."},{"mod":"m4","ch":5,"p":"62–63","q":"What are the differences in transcription initiation between prokaryotes and eukaryotes? What are general transcription factors? What are enhancers? What is the mediator complex? What role do sigma factors play in bacterial transcription?","a":"In bacteria, a sigma factor associates with RNA polymerase and directs it to bind specific promoters. In eukaryotes, at least six general transcription factors bind the promoter; transcriptional activator proteins bind enhancer sequences (often far away); the mediator complex associates with the general transcription factors and RNA Pol II, and DNA looping brings the activators into contact with it, recruiting Pol II and starting transcription."},{"mod":"m4","ch":6,"p":"63–64","q":"What is the process for transcription elongation? What are the steps of the polymerization reaction? How is transcription terminated?","a":"RNA polymerase unwinds the DNA and adds complementary ribonucleotides, which enter through a channel, to the growing RNA. Each step: the polymerase accepts an incoming ribonucleoside triphosphate only if it base-pairs correctly; the 3′–OH of the growing strand attacks the innermost phosphate of the triphosphate; pyrophosphate is released and the nucleotide is attached. Transcription continues until RNA polymerase reaches a terminator sequence, and the RNA is released (in eukaryotes, polyadenylation also plays a role in termination)."},{"mod":"m4","ch":7,"p":"65","q":"What is polycistronic mRNA? What type of cell produces primary transcripts of this type?","a":"An mRNA that carries the information for more than one gene. Prokaryotes make them."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"65","q":"What modifications occur to pre-mRNA in eukaryotes before it exits the nucleus?","a":"RNA processing: a 5′ cap is added, introns are removed by RNA splicing, and a poly(A) tail is added to the 3′ end."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What are introns and exons, and where are they found?","a":"Both are found in eukaryotic genes and their primary transcripts. Introns are sequences cut out of the primary transcript during splicing; exons are the sequences left intact and joined together in the mature mRNA."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What is the function of the 5′ cap and the 3′ poly-A tail?","a":"The 5′ cap (7-methylguanosine joined by a 5′–5′ bond) protects the mRNA from exonucleases, adds stability, and is needed for the ribosome to recognize the mRNA. The poly(A) tail (about 250 adenines) helps with transcription termination, export of the mRNA to the cytoplasm, and protection from exonucleases."},{"mod":"m4","ch":8,"p":"66","q":"What is RNA splicing, and what is the end result? What is alternative splicing?","a":"RNA splicing cuts the introns out of the primary transcript and joins the exons, giving a mature mRNA. Alternative splicing is when one gene's primary transcript is spliced in different ways, so a single gene can make different but related proteins in different cells."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What is the central dogma of molecular biology?","a":"Genetic information flows from DNA to RNA (transcription) to protein (translation).","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What defines a polypeptide chain?","a":"A linear chain of amino acids joined by peptide bonds, with a free amino group at one end (N-terminus) and a free carboxyl group at the other (C-terminus). A protein is one or more polypeptides folded into a functional shape.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What are the four levels of protein structure and how does each contribute to a protein's overall shape?","a":"Primary: the amino acid sequence. Secondary: local folding into α-helices and β-sheets, held by hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms. Tertiary: the overall 3-D shape of one polypeptide, set by interactions between R groups (hydrophobic interactions, hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges). Quaternary: two or more polypeptides joined into one functional protein.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How does the unique sequence of amino acids determine the final shape of a protein?","a":"The sequence places R groups with different properties (nonpolar, polar, charged) at specific positions. As the chain folds, hydrophobic R groups cluster inside, away from water, and R groups form hydrogen, ionic and disulfide bonds with each other. The combination of these interactions settles the chain into one stable shape.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How do peptide bonds form between amino acids?","a":"By a condensation (dehydration) reaction: the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of the next, releasing water and forming a covalent C–N peptide bond. On the ribosome, this is catalyzed by the large subunit.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How do primary and secondary structures differ in proteins?","a":"Primary structure is just the order of amino acids, held by covalent peptide bonds. Secondary structure is local folding of the backbone into α-helices or β-sheets, held by hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N–H groups (not R groups).","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What types of chemical bonds stabilize secondary structures like α-helices and β-sheets?","a":"Hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen (C=O) of one amino acid and the amide hydrogen (N–H) of another, in the polypeptide backbone.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"Why is tertiary structure important for protein function?","a":"It creates the protein's specific 3-D shape, including active sites and binding sites. Function depends on that shape, so if tertiary structure is disrupted (denaturation), the protein usually stops working.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How does quaternary structure arise and what is its biological significance?","a":"It forms when two or more folded polypeptide subunits associate through the same weak interactions (and sometimes disulfide bonds). Many proteins, like hemoglobin with its four subunits, only work as the assembled complex, and the subunits can affect each other's activity.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What roles do hydrophobic and ionic interactions play in maintaining protein conformation?","a":"Hydrophobic interactions push nonpolar R groups to cluster in the protein's interior away from water, driving folding. Ionic interactions form between oppositely charged R groups (for example, a + lysine and a – aspartate) and help hold the folded shape.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How can a single amino acid substitution drastically change a protein's function?","a":"If the new amino acid has a different R group (for example, nonpolar instead of charged), it can change folding or an active site. Sickle-cell anemia is the classic example: one glutamic acid replaced by valine in hemoglobin makes the molecules stick together and deform red blood cells.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How does the amino acid sequence determine protein function? What are the major groupings of amino acids?","a":"Sequence determines folding, folding determines shape, and shape determines function. The 20 amino acids are grouped by their R groups: nonpolar (hydrophobic), polar uncharged, and charged (acidic, negative; basic, positive), with a few special cases such as glycine, proline and cysteine.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How do hydrophobic and hydrophilic interactions influence protein folding?","a":"In water, hydrophobic R groups fold toward the interior, while hydrophilic (polar and charged) R groups stay on the surface where they interact with water. This is a major force driving a protein into its folded shape.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What is the genetic code? What is a codon? What is an anti-codon? Describe directionality of each?","a":"The genetic code is the set of rules relating three-base codons in mRNA to amino acids (64 codons, including start AUG and three stop codons; the code is redundant). A codon is three bases in mRNA, read 5′→3′. An anticodon is the complementary three bases on a tRNA; it pairs with the codon antiparallel, so it is written 3′→5′ against the codon's 5′→3′.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How do tRNAs become charged with amino acids?","a":"Enzymes called aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases attach the correct amino acid to the 3′ end of its tRNA, using energy from ATP. There is one synthetase for each amino acid, which recognizes both the amino acid and its matching tRNAs.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What role do ribosomes play in protein synthesis?","a":"Ribosomes (large and small subunits made of rRNA and protein) bind the mRNA, hold tRNAs in their A, P and E sites, match codons to anticodons, and catalyze peptide bond formation, moving along the mRNA 5′→3′.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How does mRNA direct the sequence of amino acids during translation?","a":"The ribosome reads the mRNA's codons in order from the start codon. Each codon is matched by a tRNA with a complementary anticodon carrying a specific amino acid, so the codon order sets the amino acid order.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What are the main differences between transcription and translation?","a":"Transcription copies DNA into RNA using RNA polymerase (in the nucleus in eukaryotes) and stays in the language of nucleic acids. Translation turns the mRNA's codons into a polypeptide using ribosomes and tRNAs (in the cytoplasm), changing the language to amino acids.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How is translation initiated in prokaryotes and eukaryotes?","a":"In both, the small ribosomal subunit, the initiator tRNA (carrying methionine; formyl-methionine in bacteria) and the mRNA come together at the start codon AUG, then the large subunit joins. In prokaryotes, the small subunit binds a Shine-Dalgarno sequence just upstream of the start codon. In eukaryotes, it binds the 5′ cap and scans along the mRNA until it finds the first AUG.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What are the steps of translation elongation?","a":"1) A charged tRNA whose anticodon matches the codon enters the A site. 2) The ribosome forms a peptide bond, moving the growing chain from the tRNA in the P site onto the amino acid in the A site. 3) The ribosome moves one codon along (translocation): the empty tRNA shifts to the E site and leaves, and the tRNA with the chain moves to the P site. Repeat.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How is translation terminated?","a":"When a stop codon (UAA, UAG or UGA) reaches the A site, no tRNA matches it. A release factor binds instead, the polypeptide is released from the last tRNA, and the ribosome subunits separate.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How does a protein's shape relate to its biological activity?","a":"Activity depends on shape: an enzyme's active site or a receptor's binding site must fit its partner molecule. Changing the shape, by mutation, heat, pH or denaturation, usually changes or destroys activity.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How would a DNA mutation alter protein synthesis and protein function?","a":"A mutation changes the mRNA codons. A silent mutation codes for the same amino acid (no effect); a missense mutation swaps one amino acid (effect ranges from none to severe); a nonsense mutation creates an early stop codon (a shortened, usually nonfunctional protein); an insertion or deletion can shift the reading frame and change every amino acid after it.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"What is the role of tRNA in translation?","a":"tRNA is the adapter: it carries a specific amino acid at its 3′ end and has an anticodon that base-pairs with the matching mRNA codon, so each amino acid is delivered to the right place.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How does the ribosome ensure that amino acids are added in the correct order during translation?","a":"By codon–anticodon base pairing in the A site: only a tRNA whose anticodon pairs correctly with the codon is held long enough for a peptide bond to form. Correct charging of tRNAs by aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases is the other half of the accuracy.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"Why are some proteins secreted while others remain in the cytoplasm?","a":"It depends on where they're made. Proteins with a signal sequence are made on ribosomes bound to the rough ER, enter the endomembrane system and can be secreted or sent to membranes or lysosomes. Proteins without one are made on free ribosomes and stay in the cytosol (or go to the nucleus, mitochondria and so on).","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How do signal sequences direct proteins to specific organelles?","a":"A signal sequence is a short stretch of amino acids (often at the N-terminus) that acts as an address label. For the ER, a signal recognition particle (SRP) binds the signal as it emerges from the ribosome and brings the ribosome to the ER membrane, where the protein is threaded in. Other signal sequences target the nucleus, mitochondria or chloroplasts.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."},{"mod":"m5","q":"How does alternative splicing influence the diversity of proteins produced by a single gene?","a":"By joining different combinations of exons from the same primary transcript, one gene can produce several different mRNAs and therefore several related proteins, often in different cell types.","ch":null,"p":"","note":"Based on the textbook. Module 5 notes not added yet."}],"built":"2026-10-19"};
